window.LAI_SEARCH = [{"k":"Pattern","n":"01","t":"Goodhart's Law","s":"When a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure.","u":"patterns/goodharts-law.html"},{"k":"Section","n":"01","t":"Goodhart's Law › Takeaways","x":"Check a result against a second measure that nobody optimized, not only the number everyone pushed up. A benchmark score stands in for something you actually care about. The harder anyone pushes on the stand-in, the further it drifts from the real thing. AI gets hit by this twice. People chase leaderboard numbers, and models trained on a reward chase that reward. Keep at least one measure outside the optimization loop: a held-back test, fresh data, or a direct check of the real outcome. When a score jumps, ask what improved. The capability, or the fit to the test?","u":"patterns/goodharts-law.html#takeaways"},{"k":"Section","n":"01","t":"Goodhart's Law › What it means","x":"Every eval is a proxy. Accuracy on a coding benchmark stands in for \"writes useful software.\" Win rate in a chatbot arena stands in for \"people find it helpful.\" A proxy works fine while nobody leans on it. Once the number becomes the goal, people and systems find ways to move the number that don't move the thing behind it. Manheim and Garrabrant (2018) split the problem into four variants. Regressional: the proxy and the goal only partly overlap, so picking the top scorers also picks up noise. Extremal: the relationship holds in normal ranges and breaks at the extremes. Causal: the proxy and the goal are linked only indirectly, so pushing on the proxy may not move the goal. Adversarial: someone, or something, games the metric on purpose.","u":"patterns/goodharts-law.html#what-it-means"},{"k":"Section","n":"01","t":"Goodhart's Law › The evidence","x":"It can be measured. In a synthetic setup where a fixed \"gold\" reward model stood in for human raters, Gao, Schulman, and Hilton (2023) optimized language models against a proxy reward model and tracked the gold score. It rose at first, then fell as optimization continued, and the fitted trends scaled smoothly with reward model size. More capable systems often game harder. Amodei et al. (2016) listed reward hacking as one of five concrete AI-safety problems. Pan, Bhatia, and Steinhardt (2022) built four reinforcement-learning environments with misspecified rewards and found that more capable agents often exploited them more, with some sharp capability thresholds where behavior shifted and true reward dropped. Leaderboards feel it too. Singh et al. (2025) documented how private testing on Chatbot Arena let some providers try many model variants and publish only the best. The authors identified 27 private variants they attribute to Meta in the lead-up to the Llama 4 release (attribution inferred from each model's self-identification, January to March 2025). In a controlled fine-tuning experiment, adding Arena data raised one model's win rate on ArenaHard, a test set drawn from Arena prompts, by up to 112% relative, with limited gains on other tasks. LMArena disputes parts of the analysis (its response is linked on the case studies page). It's getting more common. The International AI Safety Report 2026 notes models increasingly find loopholes that let them score well on evaluations without doing what the evaluation intended (Bengio et al., 2026).","u":"patterns/goodharts-law.html#the-evidence"},{"k":"Section","n":"01","t":"Goodhart's Law › Use it","x":"Before trusting a score jump, check a second measure nobody optimized. Keep a private holdout set that never informs training, prompt tuning, or model selection. Refresh test items on a schedule. Pair every number with a sample of real outputs that a person reads. Ask: \"What's the cheapest way to raise this number without improving the product?\" If the answer is easy, expect it to happen.","u":"patterns/goodharts-law.html#use-it"},{"k":"Section","n":"01","t":"Goodhart's Law › Where this doesn’t apply","x":"Not every target gets gamed. A measure that is hard to move without doing the real work, or one checked against separate evidence, holds up far better. The effect also depends on how hard something is optimized: in the reward-model study, the true score rose before it fell.","u":"patterns/goodharts-law.html#limits"},{"k":"Section","n":"01","t":"Goodhart's Law › Origins","x":"Economist Charles Goodhart described the idea in a 1975 paper on UK monetary policy: any observed statistical regularity tends to collapse once pressure is placed on it for control purposes. Anthropologist Marilyn Strathern gave it the popular wording in 1997 while writing about audit culture in British universities: \"When a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure.\"","u":"patterns/goodharts-law.html#origins"},{"k":"Pattern","n":"02","t":"Campbell's Law","s":"The more a number drives decisions, the more pressure there is to corrupt it, and the more likely it is to distort the work it was meant to track.","u":"patterns/campbells-law.html"},{"k":"Section","n":"02","t":"Campbell's Law › Takeaways","x":"Do not let one number decide alone: add a second, independent measure and a qualitative review. Goodhart's Law is about the measure breaking. Campbell's Law is about people and institutions bending around it. Leaderboards influence funding, press, hiring, and buying decisions. That gives everyone a reason to game them, often without anyone deciding to cheat. The damage spreads past the number. It shapes which research gets done and which products get built. Big decisions deserve several indicators plus qualitative evidence, never a single rank.","u":"patterns/campbells-law.html#takeaways"},{"k":"Section","n":"02","t":"Campbell's Law › What it means","x":"When a benchmark becomes the currency of a field, it changes behavior. Teams pick problems that score well, report the best of many runs, quietly tune on the test, and skip work that doesn't move the rank. None of this has to be fraud. Small, reasonable-looking choices add up to a distorted picture. The same thing happens inside companies. If a launch review only asks for a win rate, teams learn to deliver a win rate.","u":"patterns/campbells-law.html#what-it-means"},{"k":"Section","n":"02","t":"Campbell's Law › The evidence","x":"Scholarship bends. Lipton and Steinhardt (2019) documented troubling trends in ML papers, including failing to identify where empirical gains came from (for example, crediting a new architecture when the gains came from tuning) and presenting speculation as explanation. They call the causes uncertain, with misaligned incentives (such as pressure for short-term measures of success) as one possibility. Access is uneven. Singh et al. (2025) estimated, from each provider's share of battles played, that Google and OpenAI received about 19.2% and 20.4% of all Chatbot Arena test prompts, while 83 open-weight models together received about 29.7%. LMArena disputes parts of the analysis (its response is linked on the case studies page). More data from the arena means more ability to fit the arena. Leaderboards encode someone's values. Ethayarajh and Jurafsky (2020) argue that leaderboards reward accuracy while ignoring things practitioners care about, like model size, energy use, and fairness. A leaderboard's idea of \"best\" is not yours. Selection shapes the story. Dehghani et al. (2021) show that which benchmarks a community adopts can favor certain methods over others.","u":"patterns/campbells-law.html#the-evidence"},{"k":"Section","n":"02","t":"Campbell's Law › Use it","x":"When a number drives a real decision (a vendor, a launch, a promotion), require at least two independent measures and a qualitative review. Ask how many variants, seeds, or prompts were tried before the reported result. Watch for teams narrowing scope to whatever the metric rewards. Reward people for finding flaws in the eval, not only for moving it.","u":"patterns/campbells-law.html#use-it"},{"k":"Section","n":"02","t":"Campbell's Law › Where this doesn’t apply","x":"The pattern describes what happens under pressure and incentives, so a measure that nobody competes on or can easily game is less exposed. The leaderboard figures here describe one leaderboard, not every ranking.","u":"patterns/campbells-law.html#limits"},{"k":"Section","n":"02","t":"Campbell's Law › Origins","x":"Social scientist Donald T. Campbell stated it in a 1976 report, published in 1979: \"The more any quantitative social indicator is used for social decision-making, the more subject it will be to corruption pressures and the more apt it will be to distort and corrupt the social processes it is intended to monitor.\" He was writing about evaluating social programs, with examples from education and policing. AI leaderboards fit the pattern closely.","u":"patterns/campbells-law.html#origins"},{"k":"Pattern","n":"03","t":"The Construct Gap","s":"A benchmark measures what it measures, which may not be what its name says.","u":"patterns/the-construct-gap.html"},{"k":"Section","n":"03","t":"The Construct Gap › Takeaways","x":"A benchmark’s name is a claim, not a result: say exactly what skill the score shows, and under what conditions. Reasoning, safety, understanding, and helpfulness are constructs. Nothing measures them directly. A benchmark is one way of turning a construct into a test. Treat its name as a claim to check. Trace the chain before trusting a score: concept, definition, test items, scoring, number. Many LLM benchmarks leave links in that chain weak. In one review of 445 of them, about half of the definitions given were contested, and about half of the benchmarks presented evidence of construct validity.","u":"patterns/the-construct-gap.html#takeaways"},{"k":"Section","n":"03","t":"The Construct Gap › What it means","x":"Measurement researchers separate the thing you care about (the construct) from the thing you can observe (the measurement). Validity is how well the evidence supports the jump from one to the other. A \"reasoning\" benchmark made of multiple-choice logic puzzles measures performance on those puzzles, in that format. Whether that says anything about reasoning in general is a separate question that needs its own evidence. Wallach et al. (2025), building on Adcock and Collier (2001), lay out four levels: a background concept (the broad, fuzzy idea), a systematized concept (an explicit definition), a measurement instrument (the actual test), and the measurements it produces. Moving between levels takes systematization, operationalization, and application, and then the whole chain has to be interrogated for validity. Wallach et al. argue that making the first two levels explicit lets people debate the concept itself, separately from the numbers.","u":"patterns/the-construct-gap.html#what-it-means"},{"k":"Section","n":"03","t":"The Construct Gap › The evidence","x":"Big names, narrow tests. Raji et al. (2021) argue that benchmarks like ImageNet and GLUE are treated as measures of general ability, like a children's-book museum that claims to hold \"everything in the whole wide world,\" when any finite dataset is specific and limited. The problem is widespread. Bean et al. (2025) had 29 expert reviewers systematically review 445 LLM benchmarks from leading NLP and ML venues and found recurring patterns that undermine the validity of the claims made from them. For example, fewer than 10% used complete real-world tasks, and 16% used uncertainty estimates or statistical tests. They offer eight recommendations. Validity lives in the interpretation. Messick (1995) argued that validity isn't a property of a test. It's a property of the inferences and uses drawn from its scores, including their consequences. Mismatches cause harm. Jacobs and Wallach (2021) argue that many fairness-related harms come from mismatches between a construct, such as teacher effectiveness or risk of recidivism, and how it is measured. Claims need matching evidence. Salaudeen et al. (2025) propose a validity-centered framework: the same score can support a narrow claim and fail to support a broad one.","u":"patterns/the-construct-gap.html#the-evidence"},{"k":"Section","n":"03","t":"The Construct Gap › Use it","x":"Finish this sentence: \"This score is evidence that the system can ___ under ___ conditions.\" If you can't fill in the blanks, you don't know what the score means. Read 20 test items yourself. Would a person who aced them actually have the skill in the benchmark's name? Match the size of the claim to the evidence. \"Scores 85% on X, on this version, with this prompt format, over this many runs\" is a safe claim. \"Can reason\" is not. Check whether the test format (multiple choice, single turn, short answer) matches how the system will actually be used.","u":"patterns/the-construct-gap.html#use-it"},{"k":"Section","n":"03","t":"The Construct Gap › Where this doesn’t apply","x":"A narrow benchmark is a fine basis for a narrow claim. The gap opens when a score is used to claim something broader, such as general ability. If your claim is only that a system does these particular tasks, the score may support it.","u":"patterns/the-construct-gap.html#limits"},{"k":"Section","n":"03","t":"The Construct Gap › Origins","x":"Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl introduced construct validity in psychology in 1955. Samuel Messick's unified framework (1989, summarized in 1995) made it the center of modern validity theory. ML researchers brought these ideas into AI evaluation over the last several years. \"The Construct Gap\" is the name this site uses for the idea.","u":"patterns/the-construct-gap.html#origins"},{"k":"Pattern","n":"04","t":"The Functionality Fallacy","s":"Don't assume an AI system works. Whether it works is the first question, not a settled one.","u":"patterns/the-functionality-fallacy.html"},{"k":"Section","n":"04","t":"The Functionality Fallacy › Takeaways","x":"Ask whether an AI system works, using independent evidence on data like yours, before asking whether it is fair or safe. Debates about fairness, ethics, and safety often skip a more basic question: does the system do what it claims? Many documented cases show deployed AI systems failing at their stated task, and those failures cause harm. Vendor-reported performance is a claim. Independent validation on your own population is evidence. Put \"does it work, for whom, and how do we know?\" at the top of every review.","u":"patterns/the-functionality-fallacy.html#takeaways"},{"k":"Section","n":"04","t":"The Functionality Fallacy › What it means","x":"Raji et al. (2022) point out that scholars, the press, and policymakers often assume AI systems work and move straight to downstream concerns such as fairness or safety. Meanwhile, some systems don't work at all, some work only in narrow conditions, and some have capabilities that were overstated from the start. If nobody checks functionality, broken systems ship and nobody measures the damage.","u":"patterns/the-functionality-fallacy.html#what-it-means"},{"k":"Section","n":"04","t":"The Functionality Fallacy › The evidence","x":"Failure takes many forms. Raji et al. (2022) built a taxonomy from 283 reported cases of deployed AI failing (2012 to 2021), ranging from tasks that can't be done at all, to engineering mistakes, to failures that appear after deployment, to capabilities that were misrepresented. It shows the kinds of failure, not how often they occur. A widely used model underperformed badly. Wong et al. (2021) externally validated the Epic Sepsis Model, which was in use at hundreds of US hospitals, using 2018 to 2019 data from one academic medical center. Its area under the curve, measured per hospitalization, was 0.63, well below the 0.76 to 0.83 the developer reported in internal documentation. At the alert threshold the authors chose, it missed 67% of patients who had sepsis while generating alerts for 18% of all hospitalized patients. Practice lags need. Hutchinson et al. (2022) studied papers from computer vision and natural language processing conferences and found evaluation concentrated on a handful of methods that capture a narrow range of decontextualized behaviors. They argue this leaves gaps for real deployment contexts. Official guidance agrees. The NIST AI Risk Management Framework (2023) treats being valid and reliable as the necessary base of trustworthy AI, and its Measure function includes demonstrating that a system is valid and reliable.","u":"patterns/the-functionality-fallacy.html#the-evidence"},{"k":"Section","n":"04","t":"The Functionality Fallacy › Use it","x":"Ask for evidence the system works on data like yours, gathered by someone other than the vendor. Ask what the system does when it's wrong, and how often that happens. Define \"works\" in terms of the outcome you care about (patients treated, tickets resolved, time saved), not only model metrics. If nobody has checked, run a pilot with a real comparison group, large enough to detect the error rate you care about, before scaling.","u":"patterns/the-functionality-fallacy.html#use-it"},{"k":"Section","n":"04","t":"The Functionality Fallacy › Where this doesn’t apply","x":"The pattern is about assuming a system works, not about every system failing. Many deployed systems do what they claim. The evidence shows that failures are well documented and worth checking for, not how often they happen or that checking will always find a problem.","u":"patterns/the-functionality-fallacy.html#limits"},{"k":"Section","n":"04","t":"The Functionality Fallacy › Origins","x":"The idea comes from Inioluwa Deborah Raji, I. Elizabeth Kumar, Aaron Horowitz, and Andrew Selbst's 2022 FAccT paper, \"The Fallacy of AI Functionality,\" which also describes a \"functionality assumption.\" \"The Functionality Fallacy\" is the name this site uses, shortened from the paper's title.","u":"patterns/the-functionality-fallacy.html#origins"},{"k":"Pattern","n":"05","t":"The Metric Mirage","s":"Change the metric and you can change the conclusion.","u":"patterns/the-metric-mirage.html"},{"k":"Section","n":"05","t":"The Metric Mirage › Takeaways","x":"Re-score a headline trend with a second reasonable metric before you believe it. The same model outputs can look like steady progress or a sudden leap, depending on how you score them. All-or-nothing metrics like exact match make gradual improvement look abrupt. Cheap automatic metrics can disagree with people about the quality of individual outputs. Before believing a trend, re-score it with another reasonable metric and see if the story holds.","u":"patterns/the-metric-mirage.html#takeaways"},{"k":"Section","n":"05","t":"The Metric Mirage › What it means","x":"The metric is part of the finding. Pick a strict pass/fail score and a model that gets closer and closer to right answers looks like it's stuck at zero, then suddenly \"gets it.\" Pick a word-overlap score and a fluent, correct answer phrased differently than the reference looks wrong. Neither number is lying, exactly. Each answers a narrower question than the headline suggests.","u":"patterns/the-metric-mirage.html#what-it-means"},{"k":"Section","n":"05","t":"The Metric Mirage › The evidence","x":"Emergence can be a scoring artifact. Schaeffer, Miranda, and Koyejo (2023) argue that, for the tasks and model families they examined, many \"emergent abilities\" appear because of the researcher's choice of metric. Nonlinear or discontinuous metrics produce sharp jumps. Linear or continuous metrics applied to the same models show smooth, predictable change. Automatic metrics have narrow validity. Reiter (2018) reviewed the evidence on BLEU and found it supports using BLEU for diagnostic evaluation of machine translation systems, not for evaluating individual texts, for tasks outside machine translation, or for scientific hypothesis testing. Overlap isn't quality. Novikova et al. (2017) tested widely used word-overlap metrics on three data-to-text datasets and found they only weakly reflect human judgments of system outputs, though they performed more reliably when comparing systems overall. One metric hides tradeoffs. Liang et al. (2023) built HELM to measure seven metrics (accuracy, calibration, robustness, fairness, bias, toxicity, efficiency) on its core scenarios where possible, because accuracy alone hides the tradeoffs that matter in use.","u":"patterns/the-metric-mirage.html#the-evidence"},{"k":"Section","n":"05","t":"The Metric Mirage › Use it","x":"Report at least one continuous metric alongside any pass/fail metric. Check automatic metrics against human judgment on a sample before relying on them. When someone shows a dramatic curve, ask what the y-axis measures and what the curve looks like with a different metric. Choose metrics before seeing results, and write down why.","u":"patterns/the-metric-mirage.html#use-it"},{"k":"Section","n":"05","t":"The Metric Mirage › Where this doesn’t apply","x":"Automatic metrics are not useless. Reiter found BLEU supports diagnostic evaluation of machine translation systems. The problem is using a metric outside the range where it has been shown to work.","u":"patterns/the-metric-mirage.html#limits"},{"k":"Section","n":"05","t":"The Metric Mirage › Origins","x":"\"The Metric Mirage\" is the name this site uses, borrowing \"mirage\" from the title of Schaeffer, Miranda, and Koyejo's 2023 paper. The broader concern about automatic metrics goes back years in natural language generation research.","u":"patterns/the-metric-mirage.html#origins"},{"k":"Pattern","n":"06","t":"Benchmark Saturation","s":"Benchmarks have a shelf life.","u":"patterns/benchmark-saturation.html"},{"k":"Section","n":"06","t":"Benchmark Saturation › Takeaways","x":"When top systems bunch up near the ceiling, the test stops separating them. Switch to a harder one. Some benchmarks go from hard to solved quickly. Once top scores bunch up near the ceiling, the test stops telling systems apart. A saturated benchmark can hide real differences between models and hide the failures that remain. A high score on an old benchmark is weak evidence about what a current system can do. Plan for renewal: refreshed or held-back test sets. Dynamic test sets are another option, but researchers disagree about them.","u":"patterns/benchmark-saturation.html#takeaways"},{"k":"Section","n":"06","t":"Benchmark Saturation › What it means","x":"A benchmark is useful while it separates good from better. Say everyone scores 95% or higher. The remaining points are often noise, label errors, or quirks of the test. Progress on the leaderboard slows to a crawl while real-world gaps stay open. That's the signal to retire the test or build a harder one.","u":"patterns/benchmark-saturation.html#what-it-means"},{"k":"Section","n":"06","t":"Benchmark Saturation › The evidence","x":"Saturation is common. Ott et al. (2022) compiled results for 3,765 benchmarks in computer vision and natural language processing. In the subset with enough results to analyze, about a third of the state-of-the-art curves showed saturation or stagnation and about 43% kept improving. Many benchmarks also never saw wide adoption. \"Superhuman\" on paper, brittle in practice. Kiela et al. (2021) note that models can reach superhuman scores on benchmarks, by narrow definitions of human performance, yet still fail simple challenge examples. Their Dynabench platform keeps humans and models in the loop to keep generating hard new examples. Fixing benchmarking takes design. Bowman and Dahl (2021) argue that systems score so high on many language-understanding benchmarks that there is little room to show progress. They propose four criteria for good benchmarks (validity, reliable annotation, enough statistical power, and handling of social bias) and argue that adversarial data collection, the kind of approach Dynabench takes, does not fix the underlying problems. The last few points can be label noise. Northcutt, Athalye, and Mueller (2021) found label errors in at least 6% of the ImageNet validation set. Near the ceiling, errors in the answer key can decide rankings.","u":"patterns/benchmark-saturation.html#the-evidence"},{"k":"Section","n":"06","t":"Benchmark Saturation › Use it","x":"Check where top scores sit relative to the ceiling and to the estimated label error rate. Favor benchmarks released after the model's training cutoff, or ones that refresh regularly. For your own product eval, add new hard cases from real failures every cycle. Retire tests that no longer separate the options you're choosing between.","u":"patterns/benchmark-saturation.html#use-it"},{"k":"Section","n":"06","t":"Benchmark Saturation › Where this doesn’t apply","x":"A saturated benchmark can still serve as a regression test or confirm a basic capability. It stops being useful for telling strong systems apart. Ott et al. found that many benchmarks saturate quickly, not all of them.","u":"patterns/benchmark-saturation.html#limits"},{"k":"Section","n":"06","t":"Benchmark Saturation › Origins","x":"\"Saturation\" is standard language in AI benchmarking research. Dynamic benchmarking efforts like Dynabench were motivated in part by how quickly static benchmarks stop being useful.","u":"patterns/benchmark-saturation.html#origins"},{"k":"Pattern","n":"07","t":"Data Contamination","s":"If the test was in the training data, the score may be measuring memory, not skill.","u":"patterns/data-contamination.html"},{"k":"Section","n":"07","t":"Data Contamination › Takeaways","x":"A public benchmark score alone cannot tell you the model has not seen the test items; check fresh, private questions. Large models train on huge scrapes of the internet, and public benchmarks live on the internet. Contaminated scores overstate how well a model handles problems it hasn't seen. You usually can't inspect a model's training data, so contamination has to be tested indirectly. Fresh, private, or post-cutoff test sets are the strongest defense, if they stay private and are genuinely new.","u":"patterns/data-contamination.html#takeaways"},{"k":"Section","n":"07","t":"Data Contamination › What it means","x":"Contamination, a close relative of what researchers call leakage, happens when information from the test gets into training. In classic machine learning it's usually a pipeline bug, like normalizing data before splitting it. With large language models it's often invisible and hard to avoid, because nobody outside the lab knows exactly what went into training. A model that has seen the questions, or close paraphrases, can score well without the skill the test was built to measure.","u":"patterns/data-contamination.html#what-it-means"},{"k":"Section","n":"07","t":"Data Contamination › The evidence","x":"Fresh questions, lower scores. Zhang et al. (2024) had human annotators write GSM1k, a new set of grade school math problems matched to the popular GSM8K benchmark. Some model families dropped up to 8% in accuracy. Models that assigned higher probability to the GSM8K test items tended to show bigger gaps (Spearman rank correlation of 0.36), which suggests partial memorization, though the authors note outliers and say contamination is likely not the full story. Many frontier models showed little or no gap, which the authors say could reflect better generalization or more careful data handling, so this is a check to run, not a blanket verdict. Measure it per benchmark. Sainz et al. (2023) argue that contamination should be measured for each benchmark and model, because contaminated results lead to wrong conclusions in published research. Leakage spans fields. Kapoor and Narayanan (2023) compiled 22 earlier papers that found leakage errors in 17 scientific fields that use ML, collectively affecting 294 papers, a count the authors treat as a lower bound. They also proposed a taxonomy of leakage types. Still a current problem. The International AI Safety Report 2026 lists test questions that already appear in training data as one reason benchmark scores fail to reflect real-world use (Bengio et al., 2026).","u":"patterns/data-contamination.html#the-evidence"},{"k":"Section","n":"07","t":"Data Contamination › Use it","x":"Build a small private test set from your own data that has never been posted online, and keep it out of any vendor service that may train on your inputs. Balloccu et al. (2024) estimated that GPT-3.5 and GPT-4 were exposed to about 4.7 million samples from 263 benchmarks, partly through data sent in by users. Compare scores on public items against freshly written look-alike items. A big gap is a red flag. Check the benchmark's release date against the model's training cutoff. Treat this as a rough screen: cutoffs are approximate, and new items can be rewrites of older ones. Ask vendors what contamination checks they ran and how. In your own pipelines, split data by time, user, or source before any preprocessing.","u":"patterns/data-contamination.html#use-it"},{"k":"Section","n":"07","t":"Data Contamination › Where this doesn’t apply","x":"Many frontier models showed little or no gap on fresh questions, so contamination is a check to run, not a verdict. A small gap is not proof of no contamination either. A freshly written test set, or documentation of what training data was excluded, reduces the concern.","u":"patterns/data-contamination.html#limits"},{"k":"Section","n":"07","t":"Data Contamination › Origins","x":"Leakage has been a known hazard in statistics and machine learning for decades. The scale of web-trained language models turned it from an occasional bug into a standing concern for every public benchmark.","u":"patterns/data-contamination.html#origins"},{"k":"Pattern","n":"08","t":"Adaptive Overfitting","s":"A test set only gives an unbiased score for choices made without looking at it.","u":"patterns/adaptive-overfitting.html"},{"k":"Section","n":"08","t":"Adaptive Overfitting › Takeaways","x":"Lock a test set and use it only to decide, and do your tuning on a separate dev set. A test set gives an unbiased read only for models chosen without looking at its results. Choosing prompts, models, or settings based on test results can fit your system to that specific test. The effect has been small in the large studies so far, but you can't know for your own test without a fresh one. Separate a dev set you iterate on from a locked test set you decide with.","u":"patterns/adaptive-overfitting.html#takeaways"},{"k":"Section","n":"08","t":"Adaptive Overfitting › What it means","x":"Standard statistical guarantees assume you pick your analysis before looking at the data. Real teams don't work that way. They run the eval, tweak the prompt, run it again, try another model, run it again. Each look leaks a little information about the test set into the choices being made. After enough rounds, a high score can partly reflect how well the team learned that particular test.","u":"patterns/adaptive-overfitting.html#what-it-means"},{"k":"Section","n":"08","t":"Adaptive Overfitting › The evidence","x":"Reuse is a known risk in theory. Dwork et al. (2015) noted that standard guarantees assume the analysis was fixed before the data were examined, and proposed a \"reusable holdout\" method, built on differential privacy, that lets one holdout set be used many times while keeping estimates valid. Rebuilt test sets score lower. Recht et al. (2019) rebuilt test sets for CIFAR-10 and ImageNet following the original procedures. Accuracy dropped by 3 to 15 percentage points on CIFAR-10 and 11 to 14 points on ImageNet. The model rankings mostly held. The authors attribute the drop to a distribution gap (models generalizing worse to slightly harder images), not to adaptive overfitting, and found no sign that years of reuse had produced diminishing gains, though they note their experiments do not rule out every variant of it. Competitions held up better than expected. Roelofs et al. (2019) analyzed 120 Kaggle classification competitions and found little to no sign of adaptive overfitting from leaderboard reuse. Effects among the top entries were small (typically under 1% accuracy), and the outliers usually had problems such as small or non-i.i.d. test sets. Together with Recht et al., this means the large studies found little or no sign of the effect: the risk is clearest in theory and for small, heavily reused benchmarks, and its practical size is unsettled. Agent benchmarks are exposed. Kapoor et al. (2025) reviewed 17 AI agent benchmarks and found most lack an appropriate holdout set, and 7 have none. The authors say this has led to fragile agents that take shortcuts and overfit to the benchmark.","u":"patterns/adaptive-overfitting.html#the-evidence"},{"k":"Section","n":"08","t":"Adaptive Overfitting › Use it","x":"Keep separate splits: a dev set you iterate on, a validation set for occasional checks, and a locked test set you decide with. Log each time someone looks at locked test results. Refresh the locked test once it has driven many decisions. Treat small wins on a heavily reused test with suspicion.","u":"patterns/adaptive-overfitting.html#use-it"},{"k":"Section","n":"08","t":"Adaptive Overfitting › Where this doesn’t apply","x":"The two large empirical studies found little or no sign of it. Recht et al. attribute their drop to a distribution gap rather than adaptive overfitting, and Roelofs et al. found little to no sign of it across 120 Kaggle classification competitions. The risk is clearest in theory and for small, heavily reused benchmarks, so treat it as a risk that grows with reuse, not a constant.","u":"patterns/adaptive-overfitting.html#limits"},{"k":"Section","n":"08","t":"Adaptive Overfitting › Origins","x":"The holdout set is one of the oldest ideas in machine learning. \"Adaptive data analysis\" became its own research area in the 2010s as researchers formalized what happens when analysts reuse data across many decisions.","u":"patterns/adaptive-overfitting.html#origins"},{"k":"Pattern","n":"09","t":"The Clever Hans Effect","s":"A model can get the right answer for the wrong reason.","u":"patterns/the-clever-hans-effect.html"},{"k":"Section","n":"09","t":"The Clever Hans Effect › Takeaways","x":"Add test cases built to break the obvious shortcut, because a right answer alone does not show the right reason. Models learn whatever pattern predicts the label, including patterns nobody intended. These shortcuts look like skill on a test built the same way as the training data. They fall apart the moment the shortcut isn't there. Test with cases designed to break the likely shortcut.","u":"patterns/the-clever-hans-effect.html#takeaways"},{"k":"Section","n":"09","t":"The Clever Hans Effect › What it means","x":"Clever Hans was a horse in early 1900s Germany that seemed to do arithmetic by tapping its hoof. An investigation showed Hans was reading tiny, unconscious cues from the people asking questions. Hans was a keen reader of people, not a mathematician. Models do the same thing. If every photo of a boat has water in it, a model can learn \"water\" instead of \"boat.\" Geirhos et al. (2020) call this shortcut learning: decision rules that perform well on standard benchmarks and fail to transfer to harder conditions, like the real world.","u":"patterns/the-clever-hans-effect.html#what-it-means"},{"k":"Section","n":"09","t":"The Clever Hans Effect › The evidence","x":"The watermark detector. Lapuschkin et al. (2019) used explanation methods on image classifiers trained on the PASCAL VOC 2007 dataset and found that a Fisher-vector classifier identified horses mainly by a source tag, a copyright watermark in the corner of about one-fifth of the horse images. A deep network trained on the same data did not rely on it. Answers without the question. Gururangan et al. (2018) showed that in natural language inference datasets, a model given only the hypothesis sentence, without the premise, could predict the label far above chance. Annotators had left telltale word patterns, like negation words in contradictions. Heuristics, not understanding. McCoy, Pavlick, and Linzen (2019) built the HANS test set and found that models trained on the MNLI dataset, including BERT, performed very poorly on examples where those heuristics give the wrong answer, which suggests they rely on shallow shortcuts such as word overlap. Test behavior, not just accuracy. Ribeiro et al. (2020) introduced CheckList, a behavioral testing approach borrowed from software engineering, and found critical failures in commercial and research models that had high benchmark accuracy.","u":"patterns/the-clever-hans-effect.html#the-evidence"},{"k":"Section","n":"09","t":"The Clever Hans Effect › Use it","x":"Ask, \"What's the laziest way to score well on this test?\" Then build items where that way fails. Run a partial-input baseline. Remove the part of the input that should matter and see if accuracy stays high. High accuracy is a red flag; low accuracy does not prove there are no shortcuts (Feng et al., 2019). Create contrast pairs: minimal edits that should flip the answer, and edits that shouldn't change it. Look at attributions or reasoning for a sample of correct answers, not just wrong ones. Treat them as hints to test with contrast cases, not as proof of why the model answered (Turpin et al., 2023).","u":"patterns/the-clever-hans-effect.html#use-it"},{"k":"Section","n":"09","t":"The Clever Hans Effect › Where this doesn’t apply","x":"A model that uses a shortcut may still be good enough for your use. Shortcuts matter when conditions change or when the claim is about understanding. Behavioral tests help you tell which case you are in.","u":"patterns/the-clever-hans-effect.html#limits"},{"k":"Section","n":"09","t":"The Clever Hans Effect › Origins","x":"The horse was investigated by psychologist Oskar Pfungst (1911), and \"Clever Hans\" became shorthand in psychology for experimenter cues. Sturm (2014) used the story to frame tests of music-recognition systems that scored well without using musical knowledge, calling them \"horses.\" Lapuschkin et al. (2019) popularized the phrase \"Clever Hans predictors\" in machine learning. Geirhos et al. (2020) unified related findings under \"shortcut learning.\"","u":"patterns/the-clever-hans-effect.html#origins"},{"k":"Pattern","n":"10","t":"The Gold Standard Myth","s":"Labels and human ratings are measurements with error, not ground truth.","u":"patterns/the-gold-standard-myth.html"},{"k":"Section","n":"10","t":"The Gold Standard Myth › Takeaways","x":"Treat human labels as measurements with error: report how much raters agree. Test sets contain wrong labels. Near the top of a leaderboard, those errors can decide who \"wins.\" People disagree. For many tasks that disagreement is real information, not noise to average away. Untrained, non-expert human raters can be close to random on hard judgments. Report agreement between raters, and audit labels on the items that matter most.","u":"patterns/the-gold-standard-myth.html#takeaways"},{"k":"Section","n":"10","t":"The Gold Standard Myth › What it means","x":"Every eval compares a system's output to something treated as correct: a label, a reference answer, a human rating. That \"correct\" answer was produced by people working under time pressure, with instructions that couldn't cover every case. Some answers are wrong. Some questions have more than one defensible answer. Calling it ground truth hides all of that.","u":"patterns/the-gold-standard-myth.html#what-it-means"},{"k":"Section","n":"10","t":"The Gold Standard Myth › The evidence","x":"Answer keys have errors. Northcutt, Athalye, and Mueller (2021) found an average of at least 3.3% label errors across 10 widely used test sets, and at least 6% in the ImageNet validation set. With corrected labels, rankings between models could flip. On ImageNet, with corrected labels, ResNet-18 would outperform ResNet-50 if the share of originally mislabeled test examples rose by just 6%. Disagreement is signal. Aroyo and Welty (2015) describe seven myths of human annotation, including that every item has one right answer and that disagreement is bad. They argue disagreement is a signal, not noise: it can show that an item is ambiguous or vague, or that a worker is not doing a good job. Raters can be at chance. Clark et al. (2021) asked non-expert crowdworkers to tell GPT-3 text from human-written text. Without training they scored at chance (49.9%), and training raised accuracy only to about 55%, with no significant gain within any of the three text types they tested. This tests spotting machine authorship, not rating quality. Crowdsourced ratings can mislead. Karpinska, Akoury, and Iyyer (2021) found that Mechanical Turk workers, unlike English teachers, failed to distinguish stories from a fine-tuned GPT-2 model from human-written ones in their ratings, though their judgments improved when the two were shown side by side. Most of the 45 papers they surveyed also left out key details about how their crowdsourced ratings were collected. Practice is inconsistent. van der Lee et al. (2021) reviewed human evaluation in natural language generation, found wide variation in how it's done and reported, and proposed best-practice guidelines.","u":"patterns/the-gold-standard-myth.html#the-evidence"},{"k":"Section","n":"10","t":"The Gold Standard Myth › Use it","x":"Have two people label a sample independently and measure their agreement, with a chance-corrected measure such as Cohen's kappa, before trusting the labels. High agreement shows the labels are consistent, not that they are right. Audit labels on items where strong models disagree with the \"answer.\" Use raters with real expertise for expert tasks, and train and calibrate them. Keep the disagreement data. Report distributions, not just majority votes.","u":"patterns/the-gold-standard-myth.html#use-it"},{"k":"Section","n":"10","t":"The Gold Standard Myth › Where this doesn’t apply","x":"Human judgment is still the best reference for many tasks. The pattern says to treat labels as evidence that can contain errors, and disagreement as information. It does not say to drop human evaluation.","u":"patterns/the-gold-standard-myth.html#limits"},{"k":"Section","n":"10","t":"The Gold Standard Myth › Origins","x":"\"The Gold Standard Myth\" is this site's name for a theme that runs through annotation research, crowdsourcing studies, and human evaluation guidelines. Aroyo and Welty's \"Truth Is a Lie\" (2015) is the clearest early statement of it.","u":"patterns/the-gold-standard-myth.html#origins"},{"k":"Pattern","n":"11","t":"Prompt Sensitivity","s":"A score from one prompt is a score for that prompt.","u":"patterns/prompt-sensitivity.html"},{"k":"Section","n":"11","t":"Prompt Sensitivity › Takeaways","x":"Report a score across several reasonable prompts, with the spread, instead of from a single prompt. Small formatting changes (spacing, separators, option labels, wording) can swing LLM results by a lot. The best format for one model often isn't the best for another, so single-prompt comparisons can be unfair. Report results across several reasonable prompts, including the spread. Test with the prompts real users actually write.","u":"patterns/prompt-sensitivity.html#takeaways"},{"k":"Section","n":"11","t":"Prompt Sensitivity › What it means","x":"Traditional software gives the same result for the same input. Language models respond to surface details a person would ignore. If you evaluate two models with one fixed prompt template, you're partly measuring how well each model happens to like that template. The ranking you get might not survive a rewrite.","u":"patterns/prompt-sensitivity.html#what-it-means"},{"k":"Section","n":"11","t":"Prompt Sensitivity › The evidence","x":"Formatting alone moves scores. Sclar et al. (2024) found that several widely used open-source LLMs were extremely sensitive to subtle formatting changes in few-shot prompts, with differences of up to 76 accuracy points for LLaMA-2-13B in the worst case, and about 10 points on average across 50+ tasks and several models. The best format for one model correlated only weakly with the best format for another. They propose FormatSpread to report a range instead of one number. Single-prompt results are brittle. Mizrahi et al. (2024) analyzed 6.5 million instances across 20 models and 39 tasks and found results from single-instruction evaluation brittle. They call for evaluating with a diverse set of prompts, and for choosing metrics that fit the needs of different users. For model developers they favor the average over many prompts. For a team choosing a model for one product, they favor comparing each model at its best prompt, picked on separate held-out examples so the choice is not tuned on the test set. Implementation details matter. Biderman et al. (2024), drawing on experience maintaining a widely used LLM evaluation harness, show that small choices in prompts, answer extraction, and normalization change scores, and argue for sharing exact prompts and code.","u":"patterns/prompt-sensitivity.html#the-evidence"},{"k":"Section","n":"11","t":"Prompt Sensitivity › Use it","x":"Test several prompt variants and report the mean and range. Start with at least three to five (this guide's rule of thumb, not a figure from the studies). Use the same set of variants for every model you compare. Include messy, realistic user phrasings, not just clean templates. Save the exact prompts and scoring code alongside the results.","u":"patterns/prompt-sensitivity.html#use-it"},{"k":"Section","n":"11","t":"Prompt Sensitivity › Where this doesn’t apply","x":"Sensitivity varies by model and task, and the largest swings in the evidence come from specific models and formats. If you run one fixed, well-tested prompt, the question becomes how that prompt holds up on your real inputs.","u":"patterns/prompt-sensitivity.html#limits"},{"k":"Section","n":"11","t":"Prompt Sensitivity › Origins","x":"\"Prompt sensitivity\" is not one paper's coinage. The term describes a family of findings from research on few-shot and instruction prompting.","u":"patterns/prompt-sensitivity.html#origins"},{"k":"Pattern","n":"12","t":"Judge Bias","s":"An AI grader can have preferences, including a preference for itself.","u":"patterns/judge-bias.html"},{"k":"Section","n":"12","t":"Judge Bias › Takeaways","x":"Check an AI grader against human labels on your own task before you trust its scores. Using an LLM as a judge is fast and cheap, and in one major study a strong judge agreed with people about as often as people agree with each other, once tied votes were set aside. Judges also have known biases: which answer comes first, how long it is, and whether it sounds like the judge's own writing. Swapping answer order helps. Controlling for length and using a judge from a different developer are common precautions, but the sources cited here did not test them. Validate the judge against human labels on your task before trusting it at scale.","u":"patterns/judge-bias.html#takeaways"},{"k":"Section","n":"12","t":"Judge Bias › What it means","x":"LLM-as-a-judge means asking one model to grade or compare outputs from another. It solved a real problem: human evaluation is slow and expensive, and open-ended outputs don't have a single right answer. But a judge is a model, with its own quirks. If you don't measure those quirks, they end up baked into your results.","u":"patterns/judge-bias.html#what-it-means"},{"k":"Section","n":"12","t":"Judge Bias › The evidence","x":"Useful, with named biases. Zheng et al. (2023) found GPT-4 as a judge reached over 80% agreement with human preferences, similar to agreement between humans, when tied votes were set aside (85% versus 81% between humans on MT-Bench; with ties counted, 66% for GPT-4 against 63% to 67% between humans). They showed position bias and verbosity bias, found limited ability to grade math and reasoning questions, and flagged a possible self-enhancement bias that their data were too limited to confirm. Agreement was higher when the two answers differed clearly in quality. Order can flip verdicts. Wang et al. (2024) showed that an LLM judge's verdict could be changed by swapping the order of the candidate answers (GPT-4 tended to favor the first answer, ChatGPT the second), and proposed calibration strategies such as evaluating both orders. Some judges favor themselves. Panickssery, Bowman, and Feng (2024) found that self-preference when ranking news summaries was strongest in GPT-4, weaker and less consistent in GPT-3.5, and close to neutral in Llama 2. They also found LLMs can recognize their own outputs at above-chance rates. In fine-tuning experiments, stronger self-recognition went with stronger self-preference, though the authors note this correlation does not by itself prove cause. Validators need validating. Shankar et al. (2024) built EvalGen to help people align LLM-based graders with their own judgments, because LLM-based evaluators inherit the problems of the models they evaluate and need human validation.","u":"patterns/judge-bias.html#the-evidence"},{"k":"Section","n":"12","t":"Judge Bias › Use it","x":"Grade every pair twice with the order swapped. Treat inconsistent verdicts as ties or flags. Hand-label a few dozen examples as a first screen, and more when differences are small (this guide's rule of thumb). Measure judge-human agreement with its uncertainty, and read the disagreements before scaling up. Avoid using one of the systems being compared as the judge. Where you can, use a judge from a different developer, and check its verdicts against human labels. Give the judge a specific rubric, and check whether scores correlate with response length.","u":"patterns/judge-bias.html#use-it"},{"k":"Section","n":"12","t":"Judge Bias › Where this doesn’t apply","x":"Zheng et al. found that GPT-4 as a judge agreed with human preferences more than 80% of the time when ties were set aside (about two-thirds when ties counted), so AI judges can be useful. The biases are a reason to validate and calibrate a judge, not to avoid using one.","u":"patterns/judge-bias.html#limits"},{"k":"Section","n":"12","t":"Judge Bias › Origins","x":"\"LLM-as-a-judge\" was popularized by Zheng et al. (2023). \"Judge Bias\" is this site's umbrella name for the biases documented since.","u":"patterns/judge-bias.html#origins"},{"k":"Pattern","n":"13","t":"Criteria Drift","s":"You often figure out what 'good' means by grading outputs, so expect your criteria to change.","u":"patterns/criteria-drift.html"},{"k":"Section","n":"13","t":"Criteria Drift › Takeaways","x":"Version your grading criteria, and re-grade a fixed reference set whenever they change. In the study that named this pattern, practitioners could not fully specify their criteria before grading real outputs. In that study, grading surfaced new criteria and changed how participants read the ones they had. The study did not test fixes, but a sensible practice is to treat evaluation criteria as a living design artifact, versioned like code. When criteria change, re-grade a fixed reference set or your trend lines stop being comparable.","u":"patterns/criteria-drift.html#takeaways"},{"k":"Section","n":"13","t":"Criteria Drift › What it means","x":"If you've done qualitative research, this will feel familiar. You don't know the themes until you've read the transcripts. Evaluation works the same way. Teams sit down to define \"a good summary,\" write a few criteria, then start reading outputs and realize they care about things they never wrote down, like tone, or whether the summary admits uncertainty. The rubric grows. Some criteria turn out to matter only for certain kinds of outputs. That's healthy. The risk is pretending the criteria were fixed all along and comparing numbers graded under different rubrics.","u":"patterns/criteria-drift.html#what-it-means"},{"k":"Section","n":"13","t":"Criteria Drift › The evidence","x":"Named in a user study. In a qualitative study of nine industry practitioners building LLM-based evaluators with their EvalGen tool, Shankar et al. (2024) described criteria drift: \"users need criteria to grade outputs, but grading outputs helps users define criteria.\" Some criteria depended on the specific outputs people had seen. The study was small and started from one task, extracting entities from tweets. Start from real needs. Liao and Xiao (2023), in a position paper rather than a study of how rubrics change, argue that evaluation should be grounded in the real-world needs of the people using a system, and that methods from human-computer interaction can help close the gap between what gets measured and what matters.","u":"patterns/criteria-drift.html#the-evidence"},{"k":"Section","n":"13","t":"Criteria Drift › Use it","x":"Start with open coding. Read a few dozen real outputs (this guide's rule of thumb, not a number from the study) and note what's good and bad before writing any rubric. Version the rubric, with a date and a reason for each change. Keep a fixed reference set and re-grade it when the rubric changes. The study did not test this, but it follows from wanting comparable trend lines. Involve the people who will actually use the output in defining the criteria.","u":"patterns/criteria-drift.html#use-it"},{"k":"Section","n":"13","t":"Criteria Drift › Where this doesn’t apply","x":"This pattern rests on fewer studies than most. The term and its main evidence come from one qualitative study of nine practitioners by Shankar et al., plus related work. It matters most while you are still working out what good looks like. With settled criteria it matters less.","u":"patterns/criteria-drift.html#limits"},{"k":"Section","n":"13","t":"Criteria Drift › Origins","x":"The term comes from Shreya Shankar, J.D. Zamfirescu-Pereira, Björn Hartmann, Aditya Parameswaran, and Ian Arawjo's 2024 UIST paper, \"Who Validates the Validators?\" It was coined there. Later papers have used it, but it is a recent term, not a long-established one.","u":"patterns/criteria-drift.html#origins"},{"k":"Pattern","n":"14","t":"Once Is Not Reliable","s":"Succeeding once is not the same as succeeding every time.","u":"patterns/once-is-not-reliable.html"},{"k":"Section","n":"14","t":"Once Is Not Reliable › Takeaways","x":"For repeated or costly tasks, measure whether the system succeeds on every run, not just once. AI outputs vary from run to run. A single pass hides that. pass@k asks, \"Did it work at least once in k tries?\" pass^k asks, \"Did it work all k times?\" The second matters most when the same kind of request keeps coming back or each failure is costly. Capability gains don't automatically bring reliability gains. For anything customer-facing or agentic, measure consistency across repeated runs.","u":"patterns/once-is-not-reliable.html#takeaways"},{"k":"Section","n":"14","t":"Once Is Not Reliable › What it means","x":"Many evals run each test case once, or report the best of several attempts. That's fine for measuring whether a model can do something. It's the wrong number for deciding whether to put it in front of customers. A support agent that resolves a refund correctly 60% of the time will get it wrong for a lot of real people, even if it \"passes\" the test on a good run.","u":"patterns/once-is-not-reliable.html#what-it-means"},{"k":"Section","n":"14","t":"Once Is Not Reliable › The evidence","x":"Reliability drops fast with repetition. Yao et al. (2025) built τ-bench, which simulates conversations between simulated users and agents that have tools and policies to follow. On a single try, GPT-4o with function calling solved about 61% of retail tasks and 35% of airline tasks, under 50% overall. Their pass^k metric showed consistency was worse: in the retail domain, the chance of solving a task on all 8 of 8 trials fell to roughly a quarter (2024 models). Accuracy and reliability are different axes. Rabanser et al. (2026) proposed twelve reliability metrics across consistency, robustness, predictability, and safety. Across 15 models on two benchmarks (τ-bench and GAIA), recent capability gains produced only small improvements in reliability, though some measures, such as calibration, improved more. They used one agent setup per benchmark and temperature zero, which the authors note may overstate the reliability achievable.","u":"patterns/once-is-not-reliable.html#the-evidence"},{"k":"Section","n":"14","t":"Once Is Not Reliable › Use it","x":"Run each test case more than once, starting with at least three for stochastic outputs (this guide's rule of thumb; the τ-bench study went up to eight), and report both the average success rate and the all-runs-pass rate. Separate random, occasional failures from consistent failures on specific cases. They need different fixes. Include paraphrased versions of the same task in your test set. Set reliability thresholds based on the cost of a failure, not on what the model can currently hit.","u":"patterns/once-is-not-reliable.html#use-it"},{"k":"Section","n":"14","t":"Once Is Not Reliable › Where this doesn’t apply","x":"Consistency matters most for agents and multi-step tasks. If one good answer is enough, or a person reviews every output, a strict repeat-success metric such as pass^k may ask for more than you need.","u":"patterns/once-is-not-reliable.html#limits"},{"k":"Section","n":"14","t":"Once Is Not Reliable › Origins","x":"\"Once Is Not Reliable\" is this site's name. The pass^k metric comes from Yao et al.'s τ-bench, and Rabanser et al. draw on reliability engineering in safety-critical fields for the broader framing.","u":"patterns/once-is-not-reliable.html#origins"},{"k":"Pattern","n":"15","t":"The Cost Frontier","s":"Accuracy without cost is half a result.","u":"patterns/the-cost-frontier.html"},{"k":"Section","n":"15","t":"The Cost Frontier › Takeaways","x":"Report cost and latency next to every accuracy number, and compare systems at matched cost. You can often buy accuracy with more compute: retries, multiple samples, longer reasoning, bigger models. Comparing systems that cost very different amounts to run isn't a fair comparison. Plot accuracy against cost and latency, and look at the frontier of best tradeoffs. Simple, cheap approaches sometimes match elaborate ones.","u":"patterns/the-cost-frontier.html#takeaways"},{"k":"Section","n":"15","t":"The Cost Frontier › What it means","x":"A leaderboard that only shows accuracy invites a quiet arms race. Call the model five times and take a vote, and the score often goes up. So does the bill. For a product team, a system that's two points better and ten times more expensive might be the wrong choice. A fair comparison shows both numbers, so each reader can decide which tradeoff fits their situation.","u":"patterns/the-cost-frontier.html#what-it-means"},{"k":"Section","n":"15","t":"The Cost Frontier › The evidence","x":"Accuracy-only evaluation can produce bloated agents. Kapoor et al. (2025) argued that a narrow focus on accuracy leads to needlessly complex and costly AI agents, and that cost and accuracy should be optimized together. On HumanEval, a coding benchmark, they found that simple baselines, such as retrying a model or escalating from a cheap model to a costlier one, did as well as or better than many more complex published agents at much lower cost. In one further experiment, on the HotPotQA question-answering benchmark, joint optimization lowered cost while keeping accuracy, which the authors call a starting point rather than a general result. Efficiency belongs in the scorecard. Liang et al. (2023) include efficiency as one of HELM's seven core metrics, split into training and inference efficiency. Users care about more than accuracy. Ethayarajh and Jurafsky (2020), in a critique of NLP leaderboards, argue that leaderboards ignore costs like model size and energy use that matter in practice, and advocate reporting them.","u":"patterns/the-cost-frontier.html#the-evidence"},{"k":"Section","n":"15","t":"The Cost Frontier › Use it","x":"Record cost per task (tokens or dollars) and latency next to every accuracy number. Compare systems at matched budgets, or show the full cost-accuracy curve. Always include a cheap baseline: a single call, a smaller model, a simple retry loop. Ask whether the extra points are worth the extra cost for your specific use case.","u":"patterns/the-cost-frontier.html#use-it"},{"k":"Section","n":"15","t":"The Cost Frontier › Where this doesn’t apply","x":"If cost truly does not matter, as with a rare, high-value task, putting accuracy first can be reasonable. The evidence on cost savings comes from two agent benchmarks (HumanEval and HotPotQA) and 2024-era models, and may not carry over to every setting.","u":"patterns/the-cost-frontier.html#limits"},{"k":"Section","n":"15","t":"The Cost Frontier › Origins","x":"\"The Cost Frontier\" is this site's name. The idea of comparing options along a Pareto frontier comes from economics and engineering. Kapoor et al. (2025) made the case for it in AI agent evaluation.","u":"patterns/the-cost-frontier.html#origins"},{"k":"Pattern","n":"16","t":"No Error Bars, No Result","s":"A difference smaller than the noise has not been shown to be a difference.","u":"patterns/no-error-bars-no-result.html"},{"k":"Section","n":"16","t":"No Error Bars, No Result › Takeaways","x":"Report a confidence interval with every score, and do not claim a win that the noise could explain. Every eval score is an estimate from a limited set of questions and, often, a random process. Small test sets and small improvements frequently sit inside the margin of error. Report confidence intervals, and use paired comparisons when models answer the same questions. Decide how many test items you need before running the eval.","u":"patterns/no-error-bars-no-result.html#takeaways"},{"k":"Section","n":"16","t":"No Error Bars, No Result › What it means","x":"If a model scores 80% on 200 questions, the \"true\" score on similar questions could easily be a few points higher or lower. A rough standard error for accuracy is the square root of p(1 - p) / n. For 80% on 200 items, that's about 2.8 points, so a 95% confidence interval runs roughly 5.5 points in each direction. (For small samples or scores near 0% or 100%, use a Wilson interval instead of this rough formula.) A rival model at 83% on the same test hasn't clearly won anything. Comparing the two on the same questions can cut the noise, but a 3-point gap on 200 items still stands out only if the models disagree on roughly 3% to 5% of items or fewer, meaning they give nearly identical answers. Randomness adds more noise: sampling temperature, random seeds, and data order all shift results between runs.","u":"patterns/no-error-bars-no-result.html#what-it-means"},{"k":"Section","n":"16","t":"No Error Bars, No Result › The evidence","x":"Significance testing is often skipped. Dror et al. (2018) surveyed NLP research practice, found statistical significance testing was often missing or misapplied, and wrote a practical guide to choosing the right test. Many experiments are underpowered. Card et al. (2020) showed that many NLP experiments don't have enough data to reliably detect the small improvements typically claimed, and warned that this increases the chance of exaggerated findings. Variance rivals the gains. Bouthillier et al. (2021) studied five benchmark tasks and found that variance from sources like data sampling, weight initialization, and hyperparameter choices is not small next to the improvements typically published on them. They recommend randomizing as many sources of variation as possible. A playbook for LLM evals. Miller (2024) treats eval questions as a sample from a larger population and lays out how to compute standard errors, cluster them when questions are related, compare models with paired differences, and plan sample sizes with power analysis. Benchmarks rarely report it. Reuel et al. (2024) assessed 24 AI benchmarks against 46 best practices and found most don't report statistical significance.","u":"patterns/no-error-bars-no-result.html#the-evidence"},{"k":"Section","n":"16","t":"No Error Bars, No Result › Use it","x":"Put a 95% confidence interval next to every score. Compare models on the same items with paired tests. Cluster standard errors when items come in groups, like several questions about one document. For stochastic outputs, run multiple samples per item. This reduces run-to-run noise, not noise from which items you chose. Judge a comparison by the confidence interval for the difference between the two systems, not by whether their separate intervals overlap. If the difference's interval includes zero, the systems are not shown to differ. That is not the same as showing they are equal.","u":"patterns/no-error-bars-no-result.html#use-it"},{"k":"Section","n":"16","t":"No Error Bars, No Result › Where this doesn’t apply","x":"Error bars show noise, not whether the metric is the right one or the sample is representative. When a difference is very large next to run-to-run variation, simple reporting can be enough.","u":"patterns/no-error-bars-no-result.html#limits"},{"k":"Section","n":"16","t":"No Error Bars, No Result › Origins","x":"\"No Error Bars, No Result\" is this site's name for a basic principle of statistics that AI evaluation has been slow to adopt consistently.","u":"patterns/no-error-bars-no-result.html#origins"},{"k":"Pattern","n":"17","t":"The Averaging Trap","s":"An average score can hide exactly who the system fails.","u":"patterns/the-averaging-trap.html"},{"k":"Section","n":"17","t":"The Averaging Trap › Takeaways","x":"Report results for the groups that matter, and show the worst group next to the average. Aggregate accuracy blends easy and hard cases, common and rare groups, into one number. Serious failures often cluster in subgroups that are small in the test set and important in the world. Break results down by user group, input type, language, and difficulty. Share instance-level results when you can, so others can slice them too.","u":"patterns/the-averaging-trap.html#takeaways"},{"k":"Section","n":"17","t":"The Averaging Trap › What it means","x":"A 95% overall score can be 99% for most people and 70% for a group that makes up a small slice of the test set. The average looks great. The people in that slice have a very different experience. The more a system is used across different populations, the more likely this is, and the more it matters.","u":"patterns/the-averaging-trap.html#what-it-means"},{"k":"Section","n":"17","t":"The Averaging Trap › The evidence","x":"Gender Shades. Buolamwini and Gebru (2018) tested three commercial gender classification systems in April and May 2017 and found error rates up to 34.7% for darker-skinned women, compared to at most 0.8% for lighter-skinned men. Overall accuracy was 87.9% to 93.7%. The figures describe the systems as they were then, not today's versions. Hidden stratification in medicine. Oakden-Rayner et al. (2020) showed that medical imaging models can have relative performance differences of over 20% on some clinically important subsets. A model detecting pneumothorax (collapsed lung) had an AUC of 0.94 on X-rays that showed a chest drain, but only 0.77 on X-rays without one. Chest drains are the treatment, so the model did worst on the untreated, most urgent cases. Report the breakdown. Burnell et al. (2023), in a Science policy forum article, argue that aggregate metrics and lack of access to instance-level results limit understanding of AI systems, and call for granular reporting. Build it into documentation. Mitchell et al. (2019) proposed model cards, with a quantitative analysis section broken down by groups and conditions and reported with confidence intervals where possible.","u":"patterns/the-averaging-trap.html#the-evidence"},{"k":"Section","n":"17","t":"The Averaging Trap › Use it","x":"Define the slices that matter for your users before you look at the overall number. Report the worst-performing slice next to the average, and say how many slices you looked at. Make sure each important slice has enough examples to support its own error bars. Read failures by hand. Clusters often reveal slices you didn't think to define.","u":"patterns/the-averaging-trap.html#use-it"},{"k":"Section","n":"17","t":"The Averaging Trap › Where this doesn’t apply","x":"Breakdowns need enough examples in each group to mean anything, and slicing too finely produces noisy numbers. Choose the groups that matter for how the system will be used.","u":"patterns/the-averaging-trap.html#limits"},{"k":"Section","n":"17","t":"The Averaging Trap › Origins","x":"\"The Averaging Trap\" is this site's name. \"Hidden stratification\" comes from Oakden-Rayner et al. (2020), and \"disaggregated evaluation\" is standard language in fairness research.","u":"patterns/the-averaging-trap.html#origins"},{"k":"Pattern","n":"18","t":"The Baseline Rule","s":"A result is only as strong as the baseline it beats.","u":"patterns/the-baseline-rule.html"},{"k":"Section","n":"18","t":"The Baseline Rule › Takeaways","x":"Ask what a win was measured against, and check that the comparison got the same tuning effort. Beating a weak or untuned baseline can create the illusion of progress. A fair comparison gives the old method the same tuning effort as the new one. Always include a simple baseline: a rule, a smaller model, a non-AI process, or today's human workflow. In product work, the real baseline is how people do the task right now.","u":"patterns/the-baseline-rule.html#takeaways"},{"k":"Section","n":"18","t":"The Baseline Rule › What it means","x":"\"Our model is 20% better\" means nothing until you know better than what. New methods are often tuned heavily. Baselines are sometimes copied from earlier papers without being tuned for the new setting. The gap between them can be mostly effort, not ideas. In product settings, teams sometimes compare an AI feature to a different AI feature and forget to compare it to the plain workflow people already use.","u":"patterns/the-baseline-rule.html#what-it-means"},{"k":"Section","n":"18","t":"The Baseline Rule › The evidence","x":"Tuned old methods win. Melis, Dyer, and Blunsom (2018) found that standard LSTM language models, carefully tuned, outperformed more recent and more complex architectures on language-modelling benchmarks. Neural hype in search. In an opinion piece, Lin (2019) compared two recent neural ranking papers with well-tuned classic methods on one test collection (Robust04). He argued their baselines were weak: in one paper the gains mostly disappeared, and in the other no neural model came close to the tuned classic method. Few recommenders could be rerun. Ferrari Dacrema, Cremonesi, and Jannach (2019) examined 18 neural recommendation algorithms from top conferences (2015 to 2018). Only 7 had enough public code and data to be reproduced with reasonable effort, and 6 of those could often be outperformed by simple, well-tuned methods. The seventh did not consistently beat a well-tuned non-neural linear method. The other 11 could not be rerun, so the study says nothing about how they would have fared. Simple methods capture most of the value. Hand (2006) argued that simple classifiers often capture most of the achievable predictive power, and that the gains from sophisticated methods can vanish in real use.","u":"patterns/the-baseline-rule.html#the-evidence"},{"k":"Section","n":"18","t":"The Baseline Rule › Use it","x":"Include \"the simplest thing that could work\" and \"the current process\" in every comparison. Give baselines an equal tuning budget, and say so. Be suspicious when a new method beats baselines by a wide margin on an old benchmark. For AI features, compare against the non-AI workflow with real users.","u":"patterns/the-baseline-rule.html#use-it"},{"k":"Section","n":"18","t":"The Baseline Rule › Where this doesn’t apply","x":"A baseline is a check on a comparison, not a verdict against new methods. Where a complex method really adds value, a fair comparison will show it.","u":"patterns/the-baseline-rule.html#limits"},{"k":"Section","n":"18","t":"The Baseline Rule › Origins","x":"\"The Baseline Rule\" is this site's name for a principle that shows up in nearly every field that runs experiments. Hand's \"illusion of progress\" (2006) is an early, clear statement in supervised classification research.","u":"patterns/the-baseline-rule.html#origins"},{"k":"Pattern","n":"19","t":"The Benchmark Lottery","s":"Which benchmarks you pick can decide who wins.","u":"patterns/the-benchmark-lottery.html"},{"k":"Section","n":"19","t":"The Benchmark Lottery › Takeaways","x":"Choose your benchmarks for fit to your task, and write the list down before you see any results. Model rankings often change depending on which benchmarks are included. A method can look like a breakthrough on some tasks and ordinary on others. Choosing benchmarks after seeing results is a quiet form of cherry-picking. Pick benchmarks that match your use case, and pick them before you run anything.","u":"patterns/the-benchmark-lottery.html#takeaways"},{"k":"Section","n":"19","t":"The Benchmark Lottery › What it means","x":"There are very many benchmarks. Any given paper or product announcement reports a handful. If the handful was chosen after seeing results, the table tells you more about the selection than about the system. Even without bad intent, communities settle on benchmarks that happen to favor certain approaches, and newcomers get judged by rules that weren't written for them.","u":"patterns/the-benchmark-lottery.html#what-it-means"},{"k":"Section","n":"19","t":"The Benchmark Lottery › The evidence","x":"Named and measured. Dehghani et al. (2021) showed that across several common benchmark setups the relative ranking of methods can change substantially depending on the benchmark tasks chosen, and proposed the term benchmark lottery. Models weren't even compared on the same tests. Liang et al. (2023) found that before HELM, the 30 language models they benchmarked had on average been evaluated, under any conditions and counting earlier work, on just 17.9% of HELM's 16 core scenarios, with some prominent models sharing no scenarios at all. HELM raised that to 96.0%, with the remaining gap due to technical issues. Whose utility? In an opinion paper, Ethayarajh and Jurafsky (2020) argue that leaderboards rank models on performance alone and ignore costs that matter to practitioners, such as model size and energy use, so a leaderboard can be a poor proxy for what a given user needs. An interdisciplinary warning. Eriksson et al. (2025) reviewed about 100 studies on benchmark shortcomings and found recurring problems, including data contamination, construct validity issues, misaligned incentives, and gaming of results, in a field shaped by cultural, commercial, and competitive dynamics.","u":"patterns/the-benchmark-lottery.html#the-evidence"},{"k":"Section","n":"19","t":"The Benchmark Lottery › Use it","x":"Choose your benchmark suite before seeing any results, and write it down. Weight benchmarks by relevance to your use case, not by popularity. When reading a claim, notice which common benchmarks are missing. Look at the full results table, not just the highlighted wins.","u":"patterns/the-benchmark-lottery.html#use-it"},{"k":"Section","n":"19","t":"The Benchmark Lottery › Where this doesn’t apply","x":"A benchmark can still be the right test for a clearly defined task. The lottery shows up when one benchmark's result is treated as proof of broad superiority.","u":"patterns/the-benchmark-lottery.html#limits"},{"k":"Section","n":"19","t":"The Benchmark Lottery › Origins","x":"Mostafa Dehghani and colleagues proposed the term in their 2021 paper, \"The Benchmark Lottery.\"","u":"patterns/the-benchmark-lottery.html#origins"},{"k":"Pattern","n":"20","t":"The Reproducibility Rule","s":"An eval nobody can rerun is an anecdote.","u":"patterns/the-reproducibility-rule.html"},{"k":"Section","n":"20","t":"The Reproducibility Rule › Takeaways","x":"Treat a result you cannot rerun as an anecdote, and record what you could and could not capture. Eval results depend on details: prompts, decoding settings, data versions, scoring code, and the exact model version. Without those details, nobody can check, compare, or build on a result. Documentation standards like model cards and datasheets exist so results carry their context. Save everything needed to rerun the eval, including raw outputs.","u":"patterns/the-reproducibility-rule.html#takeaways"},{"k":"Section","n":"20","t":"The Reproducibility Rule › What it means","x":"Hosted AI models can change under the same name. Prompts get tweaked. Scoring scripts get patched. Six months later, nobody can say why the number was 82% or whether today's 79% is worse. A result you can't reproduce can't be trusted, and it can't be compared to anything.","u":"patterns/the-reproducibility-rule.html#what-it-means"},{"k":"Section","n":"20","t":"The Reproducibility Rule › The evidence","x":"NeurIPS built a program for it. Pineau et al. (2021) report on the NeurIPS 2019 reproducibility program, which introduced a code submission policy, a community reproducibility challenge, and a reproducibility checklist. There, reproducibility meant getting similar results from the same code and data. Details change scores. Biderman et al. (2024) share lessons from maintaining a widely used language model evaluation harness and show how small methodological choices shift results. They recommend sharing code, prompts, and outputs. Benchmarks fall short. Reuel et al. (2024) found most of the 24 AI benchmarks they assessed can't be easily replicated. Agents especially. Kapoor et al. (2025) report pervasive shortcomings in the reproducibility of agent evaluations, focusing on HumanEval and WebArena, and attribute them to a lack of standardization in evaluation practices. Standard formats exist. Mitchell et al. (2019) proposed model cards to document intended use and evaluation results. Gebru et al. (2021) proposed datasheets to document how datasets were created and what they're suited for.","u":"patterns/the-reproducibility-rule.html#the-evidence"},{"k":"Section","n":"20","t":"The Reproducibility Rule › Use it","x":"Record the model name and version (or date), system prompt, decoding settings, dataset version, scoring code version, and run date. Save raw outputs, not just scores. Write a one-page eval card: purpose, data, metrics, slices, and known limits. Keep a small canary set and rerun it whenever a vendor updates a model.","u":"patterns/the-reproducibility-rule.html#use-it"},{"k":"Section","n":"20","t":"The Reproducibility Rule › Where this doesn’t apply","x":"Full reproducibility is not always possible, for example with proprietary models or APIs that change. In those cases the goal shifts to documenting everything that can be shared.","u":"patterns/the-reproducibility-rule.html#limits"},{"k":"Section","n":"20","t":"The Reproducibility Rule › Origins","x":"\"The Reproducibility Rule\" is this site's name. Reproducibility has been a stated norm in science for centuries. Machine learning formalized it through checklists, model cards, and datasheets starting in the late 2010s.","u":"patterns/the-reproducibility-rule.html#origins"},{"k":"Pattern","n":"21","t":"Presence, Not Absence","s":"An evaluation can show a capability or risk exists. It can't prove one doesn't.","u":"patterns/presence-not-absence.html"},{"k":"Section","n":"21","t":"Presence, Not Absence › Takeaways","x":"Read a negative result as “not found with this effort,” and ask how hard anyone tried. A failed attempt to get a model to do something tells you about the attempt, not the model's ceiling. Better prompts, tools, fine-tuning, or more time can unlock abilities an eval missed. Safety claims built on \"we tried and it didn't\" need to say how hard someone tried. Report elicitation effort alongside every negative result.","u":"patterns/presence-not-absence.html#takeaways"},{"k":"Section","n":"21","t":"Presence, Not Absence › What it means","x":"Edsger Dijkstra (1970) made this point about software in his Notes on Structured Programming: \"Program testing can be used to show the presence of bugs, but never to show their absence!\" General-purpose AI systems make it sharper. The space of possible inputs is effectively infinite, and capabilities often show up only with the right prompt, tool, or scaffolding. A clean eval report means you didn't find the problem under the conditions you tried.","u":"patterns/presence-not-absence.html#what-it-means"},{"k":"Section","n":"21","t":"Presence, Not Absence › The evidence","x":"Evaluations for extreme risks. Shevlane et al. (2023) describe dangerous capability evaluations and alignment evaluations as key inputs to training and deployment decisions, and discuss the limits of what those evaluations can catch. Models can be made to underperform. van der Weij et al. (2025) showed that when instructed in the prompt, frontier models like GPT-4 and Claude 3 Opus could selectively underperform on dangerous-capability multiple-choice tests while keeping performance on general ones. They also fine-tuned models to hide a capability unless given a password. The authors tested whether models can do this, not whether they do it on their own. Red teaming turns up a wide range of harms. Ganguli et al. (2022) released a dataset of nearly 39,000 red team attacks on language models. The harmful outputs ranged from offensive language to subtler unethical content.","u":"patterns/presence-not-absence.html#the-evidence"},{"k":"Section","n":"21","t":"Presence, Not Absence › Use it","x":"Phrase negative results precisely: \"Under these conditions, with this effort, we didn't observe X.\" Report what elicitation was tried: prompts, tools, fine-tuning, number of attempts, and time spent. Budget for red teaming by people with real domain expertise. Revisit negative results when models, tools, or techniques change.","u":"patterns/presence-not-absence.html#use-it"},{"k":"Section","n":"21","t":"Presence, Not Absence › Where this doesn’t apply","x":"A clean result is still informative. The pattern is about wording what it means: the tests found nothing, which is weaker than showing nothing is there. The sandbagging evidence tests whether models can be made to hide a capability, not whether they do so on their own.","u":"patterns/presence-not-absence.html#limits"},{"k":"Section","n":"21","t":"Presence, Not Absence › Origins","x":"The core idea is Dijkstra's, from \"Notes on Structured Programming,\" first circulated in 1969 with a second edition in 1970. \"Presence, Not Absence\" is this site's name for applying it to AI evaluation.","u":"patterns/presence-not-absence.html#origins"},{"k":"Pattern","n":"22","t":"Evaluation Awareness","s":"A model that can tell it's being tested may not act the way it will in the real world.","u":"patterns/evaluation-awareness.html"},{"k":"Section","n":"22","t":"Evaluation Awareness › Takeaways","x":"Do not assume a model behaves in deployment as it does under test; keep monitoring after launch. Frontier models can often tell evaluation prompts apart from real use. If behavior changes under test, evaluation results stop predicting deployment behavior. Scenarios that look artificial are easier to spot. Pair pre-launch tests with monitoring of real use.","u":"patterns/evaluation-awareness.html#takeaways"},{"k":"Section","n":"22","t":"Evaluation Awareness › What it means","x":"People act differently when they know they're being watched. Models trained on huge amounts of text, including text about AI evaluations, can pick up on the telltale signs of a test: multiple-choice formats, synthetic or contrived scenarios, and tasks that look like known benchmarks. If a model responds to those cues, even without any intent in the human sense, the eval measures test behavior instead of real behavior.","u":"patterns/evaluation-awareness.html#what-it-means"},{"k":"Section","n":"22","t":"Evaluation Awareness › The evidence","x":"Models are good at spotting tests. Needham et al. (2025) built a benchmark of 1,000 prompts and transcripts from 61 datasets, covering public benchmarks, real deployment interactions, and agent trajectories. Frontier models identified evaluations clearly better than chance. Gemini 2.5 Pro reached an AUC of 0.83 (0.5 is chance), compared to 0.92 for a human baseline, which was the paper's own authors and which they call an approximate upper bound. Models were better at this in agentic settings than in chat. The benchmark tests whether models can classify transcripts, not whether they change their behavior when they do. Recognized at the international level. The International AI Safety Report 2026 notes it has become more common for models to distinguish test settings from real-world deployment and to exploit loopholes in evaluations, which means dangerous capabilities could go undetected before deployment (Bengio et al., 2026). The capacity to underperform exists. van der Weij et al. (2025) showed that models can be prompted or fine-tuned to underperform on specific multiple-choice evaluations. That shows an evaluation can be gamed when a model is told to. It does not show that models detect tests or do this on their own.","u":"patterns/evaluation-awareness.html#the-evidence"},{"k":"Section","n":"22","t":"Evaluation Awareness › Use it","x":"Build eval scenarios from real, messy usage instead of obviously synthetic setups. Compare behavior in evals against behavior in real use, with consent and privacy safeguards. Track when models comment on the possibility that they're being tested. For high-stakes behavior, don't rely on pre-launch evals alone. Monitor after launch.","u":"patterns/evaluation-awareness.html#use-it"},{"k":"Section","n":"22","t":"Evaluation Awareness › Where this doesn’t apply","x":"This is a young area and several findings are preprints. A model that can identify a test is not proof that it behaves differently on tests, and the evidence here shows the capacity, not how often it matters. Some of what models recognize may be public benchmarks they have seen, not artificial setups.","u":"patterns/evaluation-awareness.html#limits"},{"k":"Section","n":"22","t":"Evaluation Awareness › Origins","x":"\"Evaluation awareness\" is the term used in recent AI safety research, for example by Needham et al. (2025). It's a young area, and several key findings are preprints, so expect this page to change.","u":"patterns/evaluation-awareness.html#origins"},{"k":"Pattern","n":"23","t":"Distribution Shift","s":"Performance measured on one population doesn't automatically carry over to another.","u":"patterns/distribution-shift.html"},{"k":"Section","n":"23","t":"Distribution Shift › Takeaways","x":"Test a system on data from the people, places, and period where it will actually run. A test set is a snapshot of certain people, places, times, and conditions. Change any of those and performance can drop, sometimes sharply. Even careful rebuilds of the same benchmark produce lower scores. Evaluate on data from the setting where the system will run, and keep checking over time.","u":"patterns/distribution-shift.html#takeaways"},{"k":"Section","n":"23","t":"Distribution Shift › What it means","x":"Models learn patterns from the data they're trained on and get tested on data that usually looks a lot like it. The real world rarely cooperates. A new hospital has different patients and equipment. A new market uses different language. Next year's users behave differently than last year's. Each change is a shift, and a benchmark score doesn't tell you how the system handles it.","u":"patterns/distribution-shift.html#what-it-means"},{"k":"Section","n":"23","t":"Distribution Shift › The evidence","x":"Same recipe, lower scores. Recht et al. (2019) rebuilt the CIFAR-10 and ImageNet test sets by following the original collection process. Accuracy fell by 3 to 15 percentage points on CIFAR-10 and 11 to 14 points on ImageNet. The authors traced the drop to the new test sets being slightly harder, not to overfitting to the old ones. Engstrom et al. (2020) argued that statistical bias in how the new ImageNet set was built explains most of its drop, so the true size of the shift is debated. Real-world shifts, measured. Koh et al. (2021) built WILDS, a benchmark of ten datasets with real distribution shifts across hospitals, cameras, regions, and time. Models showed large gaps between in-distribution and out-of-distribution performance. A caution from a clinical model. Wong et al. (2021) tested a widely deployed sepsis model at one health system and found an AUC of 0.63, against 0.76 to 0.83 in the developer's documentation. They attribute much of the gap to how they handled the timing of sepsis predictions, not to a different patient population. It shows why a vendor's reported figure shouldn't be assumed to hold at your site, but it is not a clean case of distribution shift. Drift erodes gains. Hand (2006) argued that population drift over time is one reason gains from sophisticated models shrink in practice.","u":"patterns/distribution-shift.html#the-evidence"},{"k":"Section","n":"23","t":"Distribution Shift › Use it","x":"Ask where and when the eval data came from, and compare that to your actual users. Split test results by site, time period, or source to see how performance moves. Run a local validation before rolling out to a new market, language, or customer segment. Monitor production for drift and re-evaluate on a schedule.","u":"patterns/distribution-shift.html#use-it"},{"k":"Section","n":"23","t":"Distribution Shift › Where this doesn’t apply","x":"Not every change in data hurts. In Recht et al., model rankings mostly held, and follow-up work suggests part of the ImageNet drop came from how the new test set was sampled. The question is whether your deployment differs from the test data in ways the model is sensitive to.","u":"patterns/distribution-shift.html#limits"},{"k":"Section","n":"23","t":"Distribution Shift › Origins","x":"Distribution shift, also called dataset shift, is a long-standing problem in statistics and machine learning. It is comparable to external validity in the social and medical sciences, which asks whether findings carry over to other settings.","u":"patterns/distribution-shift.html#origins"},{"k":"Pattern","n":"24","t":"The Lab-to-Field Gap","s":"A system that works in the lab can fail in the places people actually use it.","u":"patterns/the-lab-to-field-gap.html"},{"k":"Section","n":"24","t":"The Lab-to-Field Gap › Takeaways","x":"Watch a system in real use, with real workflows and people, before you call it validated. Real use adds lighting, bandwidth, workflows, time pressure, incentives, and people. Model accuracy is one layer. How people interact with the system, and its wider effects, are layers too. Many AI failures are sociotechnical. The model does what it was built to do, and the situation defeats it. Watch the system in use, in context, before calling it validated.","u":"patterns/the-lab-to-field-gap.html#takeaways"},{"k":"Section","n":"24","t":"The Lab-to-Field Gap › What it means","x":"Lab evaluation holds everything constant except the model. The field holds nothing constant. The value of an AI system comes from the whole setup: the model, the interface, the workflow, the people, and the organization around them. Evaluating only the model is like usability testing only the backend.","u":"patterns/the-lab-to-field-gap.html#what-it-means"},{"k":"Section","n":"24","t":"The Lab-to-Field Gap › The evidence","x":"A deployed system meets real clinics. Beede et al. (2020) studied a deep learning system for detecting diabetic retinopathy through observation and interviews with nurses at 11 clinics in Thailand where it was in use. They found that socio-environmental factors, such as lighting, affected model performance, nursing workflows, and the patient experience, and that the system's data-quality thresholds clashed with the quality of images those clinics could produce. Three layers of evaluation. Weidinger et al. (2023) propose evaluating generative AI at the capability layer, the human interaction layer, and the systemic impact layer, and note that most safety evaluation so far has focused on the first. Narrow the sociotechnical gap. Liao and Xiao (2023) frame evaluation as narrowing the gap between what gets measured and what real people need from a system. Abstraction traps. Selbst et al. (2019) describe traps that come from abstracting away social context, including assuming a solution built for one context will work in another. A name from the International AI Safety Report. The International AI Safety Report 2026 describes an \"evaluation gap\": performance on pre-deployment tests does not reliably predict real-world usefulness or risk (Bengio et al., 2026). Narrow evaluation practice. Hutchinson et al. (2022), studying papers at major computer-vision and language-processing conferences, found that evaluations concentrate on a narrow range of decontextualized predictive behaviors, using a handful of methods, metrics, and test distributions.","u":"patterns/the-lab-to-field-gap.html#the-evidence"},{"k":"Section","n":"24","t":"The Lab-to-Field Gap › Use it","x":"Do field observation. Sit with people using the system in their actual environment. Evaluate the workflow, not just the model: time to complete, error recovery, handoffs. Pilot in a small number of real sites before scaling. Define success with the people affected, including people who never touch the interface. Bring in UX research methods like contextual inquiry and diary studies. They are established ways to study how people use a system in context.","u":"patterns/the-lab-to-field-gap.html#use-it"},{"k":"Section","n":"24","t":"The Lab-to-Field Gap › Where this doesn’t apply","x":"Lab results still carry information. The gap is about what they leave out, such as context, workflows, and people. Findings from one field setting, like clinics in Thailand, may not transfer to yours either.","u":"patterns/the-lab-to-field-gap.html#limits"},{"k":"Section","n":"24","t":"The Lab-to-Field Gap › Origins","x":"\"The Lab-to-Field Gap\" is this site's name. Related terms include the socio-technical gap (Liao and Xiao, 2023) and the \"evaluation gap\" (International AI Safety Report 2026). They overlap but are not identical.","u":"patterns/the-lab-to-field-gap.html#origins"},{"k":"Pattern","n":"25","t":"The Team Is the System","s":"When people work with AI, evaluate the person and the AI together, not the AI alone.","u":"patterns/the-team-is-the-system.html"},{"k":"Section","n":"25","t":"The Team Is the System › Takeaways","x":"If people will act on an AI’s output, test the person and the AI together, including whether people accept wrong advice. A strong model doesn't guarantee a strong human-AI team. In a large meta-analysis of studies from 2020 to mid-2023, human-AI combinations did worse on average than the best of humans or AI alone, with losses on decision tasks and gains on content-creation tasks. Explanations can make people more likely to accept AI advice, right or wrong, without making the team more accurate. Measure overreliance (accepting wrong AI advice) and underreliance (ignoring right advice).","u":"patterns/the-team-is-the-system.html#takeaways"},{"k":"Section","n":"25","t":"The Team Is the System › What it means","x":"Most AI products put a person in the loop. That person decides whether to accept, edit, or ignore what the AI suggests. So the thing that produces outcomes is the pair, not the model. A model that's 90% accurate paired with a person who can't tell when it's wrong gains little from the pairing. If the person accepts everything, the team is only as good as the model. If they sometimes override answers the model had right, it is worse.","u":"patterns/the-team-is-the-system.html#what-it-means"},{"k":"Section","n":"25","t":"The Team Is the System › The evidence","x":"Combinations often underperform. Vaccaro, Almaatouq, and Malone (2024) analyzed 106 experimental studies with 370 effect sizes, published between January 2020 and June 2023. On average, human-AI combinations performed worse than the best of humans or AI alone (Hedges' g = -0.23). Combinations lost ground on decision-making tasks and gained on content creation tasks. When humans outperformed the AI alone, combining helped. When the AI outperformed humans alone, combining hurt. Explanations increase acceptance, right or wrong. Bansal et al. (2021) found, in studies where the AI was about as accurate as the people, that AI explanations increased the chance people accepted the AI's recommendation regardless of whether it was correct. AI assistance did produce some complementary gains, but explanations didn't add to them. Friction can help. Buçinca, Malaya, and Gajos (2021) found, in an experiment with 199 participants, that cognitive forcing functions, which make people stop and think before accepting an AI suggestion, reduced overreliance compared with simple explanations. People rated those designs less favorably, and the benefit was larger for people with higher Need for Cognition, so it helped some people more than others. An old problem. Parasuraman and Riley (1997) described use, misuse, disuse, and abuse of automation decades before generative AI. Design guidance. Amershi et al. (2019) proposed 18 guidelines for human-AI interaction, such as making clear how well the system can do what it does, and checked them with 49 design practitioners against 20 AI-infused products. They are design guidance, not a measured effect on team performance.","u":"patterns/the-team-is-the-system.html#the-evidence"},{"k":"Section","n":"25","t":"The Team Is the System › Use it","x":"When possible, compare three conditions: human alone, AI alone, and human with AI. Measure accuracy specifically on cases where the AI is wrong. That's where overreliance shows up. Track time, effort, and confidence, not just final accuracy. Test design choices like explanations, confidence displays, and forcing functions as experiments, not assumptions.","u":"patterns/the-team-is-the-system.html#use-it"},{"k":"Section","n":"25","t":"The Team Is the System › Where this doesn’t apply","x":"The evidence is not uniform. Human-AI combinations gained on content creation tasks, and they helped when humans outperformed the AI alone. The loss is concentrated in particular task types. The meta-analysis covers studies published between January 2020 and June 2023, so newer assistants may behave differently.","u":"patterns/the-team-is-the-system.html#limits"},{"k":"Section","n":"25","t":"The Team Is the System › Origins","x":"\"The Team Is the System\" is this site's name. The research draws on decades of human factors work on automation, plus newer human-AI interaction studies.","u":"patterns/the-team-is-the-system.html#origins"},{"k":"Pattern","n":"26","t":"The Jagged Frontier","s":"AI capability is uneven, and its edges don't always line up with what people find hard.","u":"patterns/the-jagged-frontier.html"},{"k":"Section","n":"26","t":"The Jagged Frontier › Takeaways","x":"AI can fail on tasks that look easy to people, so test each of your own tasks directly. AI can excel at a task that seems hard and fail at one that seems easy. The edge is hard to see, so people can end up carrying trust from tasks inside it to tasks outside it. Inside the frontier, AI help improved speed and quality. On a task built to sit outside it, AI help made people less accurate. Map the frontier for your own tasks with direct tests. Don't infer it from general benchmarks.","u":"patterns/the-jagged-frontier.html#takeaways"},{"k":"Section","n":"26","t":"The Jagged Frontier › What it means","x":"Human skill tends to be smooth: someone who can write a strong strategy memo can usually do the simpler tasks around it. AI capability is jagged. The same model can draft a solid analysis and then botch a detail any junior analyst would catch. General benchmark scores flatten this into one number, which makes them a poor guide to which of your tasks are safe to hand off.","u":"patterns/the-jagged-frontier.html#what-it-means"},{"k":"Section","n":"26","t":"The Jagged Frontier › The evidence","x":"Field experiment with consultants. Dell'Acqua et al. (2023) ran two preregistered randomized experiments with 758 Boston Consulting Group consultants, each person in only one. Each experiment had a no-AI group and two GPT-4 groups: GPT-4 alone, and GPT-4 with a prompt-engineering overview. In the first, 385 consultants did 18 realistic tasks chosen to be inside GPT-4's abilities. Those using GPT-4 completed 12.2% more tasks on average, finished 25.1% faster (timed on the first 17 tasks), and produced work that human graders rated about 38% to 43% higher in quality. In the second, 373 consultants did one business problem deliberately designed so that GPT-4 would err. On it, 84.5% of the no-AI group got a correct answer, against about 60% and 70% of the two AI groups, an average gap of 19 percentage points. Those figures are from the 2023 working paper. The published version (Dell'Acqua et al., 2026) reports the same speed and task-completion figures, describes quality as significantly improved without giving a percentage, and words the outside-the-frontier result as 19% less likely to be correct. Task type matters. Vaccaro, Almaatouq, and Malone (2024), in a meta-analysis that is not about the jagged frontier itself, found human-AI combinations tended to lose ground on decision tasks and gain on content-creation tasks, another sign that value depends heavily on which task you're looking at. An older version of the idea. Hans Moravec (1988) observed that it's comparatively easy to get computers to perform at an adult level on intelligence tests or checkers, and hard to give them the perception and mobility skills of a one-year-old.","u":"patterns/the-jagged-frontier.html#the-evidence"},{"k":"Section","n":"26","t":"The Jagged Frontier › Use it","x":"List the tasks in your workflow and test the AI on each one directly, including the ones that seem trivial. Share the map with users: where to rely on the AI and where to double-check. Re-map after model updates. The frontier moves. Design interfaces that make uncertainty visible near the edges.","u":"patterns/the-jagged-frontier.html#use-it"},{"k":"Section","n":"26","t":"The Jagged Frontier › Where this doesn’t apply","x":"The headline numbers come from two experiments at one firm with one model, GPT-4 in spring 2023. The outside-the-frontier result is a single task built to make GPT-4 err, so it shows what can go wrong, not how often it does. The frontier moves as models change. Treat it as a reason to test task by task, not as a fixed map.","u":"patterns/the-jagged-frontier.html#limits"},{"k":"Section","n":"26","t":"The Jagged Frontier › Origins","x":"The term comes from Fabrizio Dell'Acqua, Ethan Mollick, Karim Lakhani, and colleagues' field experiment, first released as a Harvard Business School working paper in 2023 and published in Organization Science in 2026.","u":"patterns/the-jagged-frontier.html#origins"},{"k":"Rubric","n":"A1","t":"The system presents options, evidence, or analysis rather than silently executing irreversible actions on the user's behalf.","s":"Agency · surface, don't decide","u":"design-rubric.html#A1"},{"k":"Rubric","n":"A2","t":"A human can see what the AI is about to do before it happens, not only after.","s":"Agency · surface, don't decide","u":"design-rubric.html#A2"},{"k":"Rubric","n":"A3","t":"High-stakes actions (financial, legal, personal data, compliance, employment) always route through explicit human confirmation.","s":"Agency · surface, don't decide","u":"design-rubric.html#A3"},{"k":"Rubric","n":"T1","t":"A confidence level or certainty signal is shown, not just a final answer.","s":"Transparency · reasoning is legible","u":"design-rubric.html#T1"},{"k":"Rubric","n":"T2","t":"The reasoning behind a suggestion is visible in plain language, not buried in a tooltip or absent entirely.","s":"Transparency · reasoning is legible","u":"design-rubric.html#T2"},{"k":"Rubric","n":"T3","t":"A user can trace an output back to the source data or logic that produced it.","s":"Transparency · reasoning is legible","u":"design-rubric.html#T3"},{"k":"Rubric","n":"H1","t":"The system has an explicit \"can't determine this\" state, rather than guessing with false confidence.","s":"Honesty · limits are disclosed","u":"design-rubric.html#H1"},{"k":"Rubric","n":"H2","t":"Edge cases and low-confidence outputs are flagged distinctly from high-confidence ones, not blended together in the same visual treatment.","s":"Honesty · limits are disclosed","u":"design-rubric.html#H2"},{"k":"Rubric","n":"H3","t":"Product or marketing copy around this feature doesn't oversell what the AI can reliably do.","s":"Honesty · limits are disclosed","u":"design-rubric.html#H3"},{"k":"Rubric","n":"E1","t":"The design was tested with assistive technology (screen reader, keyboard-only, voice control), not just visually reviewed.","s":"Equity · works across different users","u":"design-rubric.html#E1"},{"k":"Rubric","n":"E2","t":"The system accounts for different user contexts (expert vs. novice, high-stakes vs. routine) rather than a single path for everyone.","s":"Equity · works across different users","u":"design-rubric.html#E2"},{"k":"Rubric","n":"E3","t":"Output has been checked for systematic bias against any user group, language, region, or edge-case identity.","s":"Equity · works across different users","u":"design-rubric.html#E3"},{"k":"Rubric","n":"R1","t":"The AI measurably reduces steps, tools, or time, not just visual clutter while the underlying complexity remains.","s":"Real reduction · not hidden complexity","u":"design-rubric.html#R1"},{"k":"Rubric","n":"R2","t":"Automation is reversible, a user can undo, edit, or override without starting the whole task over.","s":"Real reduction · not hidden complexity","u":"design-rubric.html#R2"},{"k":"Rubric","n":"R3","t":"The feature has been validated with real task-timing or usage data, not only a demo or stakeholder walkthrough.","s":"Real reduction · not hidden complexity","u":"design-rubric.html#R3"},{"k":"Rubric","n":"F1","t":"There's a clear, designed path for what happens when the AI is wrong, not just a generic error state.","s":"Failure design · graceful degradation","u":"design-rubric.html#F1"},{"k":"Rubric","n":"F2","t":"Unfixable or ambiguous cases are flagged explicitly and don't silently block or corrupt unrelated work.","s":"Failure design · graceful degradation","u":"design-rubric.html#F2"},{"k":"Rubric","n":"F3","t":"A human reviewer can always intervene without needing engineering support to do it.","s":"Failure design · graceful degradation","u":"design-rubric.html#F3"},{"k":"Review","n":"D1.1","t":"Users are told they are interacting with an AI system, clearly and at or before first interaction.","s":"Capability disclosure · AI Act Art. 50, HAX G1–G18","u":"readiness-review.html#D1.1"},{"k":"Review","n":"D1.2","t":"Expected reliability is communicated in terms the user can act on, not as an unqualified capability claim.","s":"Capability disclosure · HAX G1–G18, AI Act Art. 13, NIST AI 100-1","u":"readiness-review.html#D1.2"},{"k":"Review","n":"D1.3","t":"Scope boundaries are stated: the interface communicates what the system is not for.","s":"Capability disclosure · HAX G1–G18, PAIR Guidebook, AI Act Art. 13","u":"readiness-review.html#D1.3"},{"k":"Review","n":"D1.4","t":"Expectation-setting is staged across the experience rather than front-loaded into a single disclaimer.","s":"Capability disclosure · PAIR Guidebook","u":"readiness-review.html#D1.4"},{"k":"Review","n":"D2.1","t":"Uncertainty is expressed per output and reflects actual model confidence rather than a fixed decorative label.","s":"Output legibility · NIST AI 100-1, HAX G1–G18, NIST AI 600-1","u":"readiness-review.html#D2.1"},{"k":"Review","n":"D2.2","t":"The reason for a given output is available at the point of decision, in plain language.","s":"Output legibility · HAX G1–G18, AI Act Art. 13, NIST AI 100-1","u":"readiness-review.html#D2.2"},{"k":"Review","n":"D2.3","t":"Output is traceable to the inputs, records, or rules that produced it.","s":"Output legibility · NIST AI 100-1, ISO/IEC 42001, AI Act Art. 13","u":"readiness-review.html#D2.3"},{"k":"Review","n":"D2.4","t":"Confidence signals and explanations are conveyed non-visually as well as visually.","s":"Output legibility · WCAG 2.2, AI Act Art. 50, W3C AI accessibility draft","u":"readiness-review.html#D2.4"},{"k":"Review","n":"D3.1","t":"The user can see what the system proposes to do before it takes effect.","s":"Human agency and oversight · AI Act Art. 14, HAX G1–G18","u":"readiness-review.html#D3.1"},{"k":"Review","n":"D3.2","t":"A person can disregard, override, or reverse the output without engineering assistance.","s":"Human agency and oversight · AI Act Art. 14, HAX G1–G18","u":"readiness-review.html#D3.2"},{"k":"Review","n":"D3.3","t":"Consequential actions require explicit human confirmation; the system does not execute them silently.","s":"Human agency and oversight · ","u":"readiness-review.html#D3.3"},{"k":"Review","n":"D3.4","t":"The design actively counters automation bias rather than encouraging uncritical acceptance.","s":"Human agency and oversight · AI Act Art. 14, Parasuraman 1997, Mosier 1996, NIST AI 600-1","u":"readiness-review.html#D3.4"},{"k":"Review","n":"D4.1","t":"An explicit \"cannot determine\" state exists and is structurally distinct from a confident answer.","s":"Uncertainty and failure design · PAIR Guidebook, HAX G1–G18, NIST AI 600-1","u":"readiness-review.html#D4.1"},{"k":"Review","n":"D4.2","t":"Low-confidence output receives different visual and interaction treatment from high-confidence output.","s":"Uncertainty and failure design · HAX G1–G18, PAIR Guidebook","u":"readiness-review.html#D4.2"},{"k":"Review","n":"D4.3","t":"A designed recovery path exists for AI-specific errors, separate from generic system error handling.","s":"Uncertainty and failure design · PAIR Guidebook, HAX G1–G18","u":"readiness-review.html#D4.3"},{"k":"Review","n":"D4.4","t":"Failure is contained: one bad output does not block, corrupt, or silently alter unrelated work.","s":"Uncertainty and failure design · NIST AI 100-1, PAIR Guidebook","u":"readiness-review.html#D4.4"},{"k":"Review","n":"D5.1","t":"The AI-specific interface has been tested with assistive technology, not only reviewed visually or inherited from a component library.","s":"Equity and accessibility · WCAG 2.2, W3C AI accessibility draft","u":"readiness-review.html#D5.1"},{"k":"Review","n":"D5.2","t":"Performance disparities across user subgroups, languages, or regions have been measured rather than assumed absent.","s":"Equity and accessibility · NIST AI 100-1, NIST AI 600-1, AI Act Art. 13, HAX G1–G18","u":"readiness-review.html#D5.2"},{"k":"Review","n":"D5.3","t":"AI-generated accessibility content is human-reviewed before it is relied upon.","s":"Equity and accessibility · W3C AI accessibility draft, WCAG 2.2","u":"readiness-review.html#D5.3"},{"k":"Review","n":"D5.4","t":"The experience adapts to user context and stakes rather than offering one undifferentiated path.","s":"Equity and accessibility · HAX G1–G18, PAIR Guidebook","u":"readiness-review.html#D5.4"},{"k":"Review","n":"D6.1","t":"The efficiency or quality claim is measured against a real pre-AI baseline, not estimated.","s":"Demonstrated value · NIST AI 100-1, ISO/IEC 42001","u":"readiness-review.html#D6.1"},{"k":"Review","n":"D6.2","t":"Complexity is genuinely removed rather than displaced into a downstream step, another team, or a later moment.","s":"Demonstrated value · NIST AI 100-1, PAIR Guidebook","u":"readiness-review.html#D6.2"},{"k":"Review","n":"D6.3","t":"A granular feedback mechanism exists and demonstrably routes into system improvement.","s":"Demonstrated value · HAX G1–G18, PAIR Guidebook","u":"readiness-review.html#D6.3"},{"k":"Review","n":"D6.4","t":"Post-deployment monitoring is defined, with a named owner and a threshold that triggers action.","s":"Demonstrated value · NIST AI 100-1, ISO/IEC 42001","u":"readiness-review.html#D6.4"},{"k":"Review","n":"D7.1","t":"Known limitations are documented in a form that actually reaches the people who deploy and use the system.","s":"Accountability record · AI Act Art. 13, ISO/IEC 42001","u":"readiness-review.html#D7.1"},{"k":"Review","n":"D7.2","t":"A named human owner is accountable for this feature's behavior in production.","s":"Accountability record · NIST AI 100-1, ISO/IEC 42001","u":"readiness-review.html#D7.2"},{"k":"Review","n":"D7.3","t":"An impact assessment covering affected individuals and groups was completed before launch.","s":"Accountability record · ISO/IEC 42001, NIST AI 100-1","u":"readiness-review.html#D7.3"},{"k":"Section","n":"Page","t":"Sample size and error bars","s":"Confidence intervals, margin of error, and how many test items you need to compare two AI systems.","u":"sample-size.html"},{"k":"Section","n":"Page","t":"AI evaluation case studies","s":"Documented cases mapped to the patterns, each with a primary source.","u":"case-studies.html"},{"k":"Section","n":"Case","t":"Case: Private variants on a public leaderboard","x":"Singh et al. (2025) studied Chatbot Arena, a public leaderboard that ranks models by votes from users. They report that some providers could test several model variants privately before release and choose which scores to publish. The most extreme case they identify is Meta, which they observed testing as many as 27 private variants in a single month before the Llama 4 release. They also estimate that Google and OpenAI received about 19.2% and 20.4% of all Arena test prompts, while 83 open-weight models together received about 29.7% of the total data. In a controlled experiment, fine-tuning a model on Arena-style data raised its win rate against a baseline model on an Arena-derived benchmark (ArenaHard) by up to 112% in relative terms, while performance on a broader benchmark (MMLU) slightly declined. The paper appeared in the NeurIPS 2025 Datasets and Benchmarks Track; the figures here are from the arXiv version (v2). Ask how many variants were tried before the reported one, and whether the score was confirmed on a second measure that nobody optimized. A published result should say how many candidates it was selected from.","u":"case-studies.html#arena-private-variants"},{"k":"Section","n":"Case","t":"Case: Fresh grade-school math problems, lower scores","x":"Zhang et al. (2024) had human annotators write GSM1k, a new set of grade-school math problems built to match the popular GSM8K benchmark in style and difficulty. Some model families scored up to 8% lower on GSM1k than on GSM8K. Models that assigned higher probability to the GSM8K test items showed bigger gaps (Spearman rank correlation of 0.36), which the authors take to suggest partial memorization, though they note outliers and say contamination is likely not the full story. Many frontier models showed little or no gap, which the authors say could reflect better generalization or more careful data handling. Build a small private set of look-alike items that has never been posted online, and compare scores on public and private items. A large gap is the warning sign; a small gap is some reassurance, not proof.","u":"case-studies.html#gsm1k"},{"k":"Section","n":"Case","t":"Case: A screening tool studied in eleven clinics","x":"Beede et al. (2020) studied a deep learning system for detecting diabetic eye disease through observation and interviews with nurses across eleven clinics in Thailand. They found that several socio-environmental factors affected model performance, nursing workflows, and the patient experience. They describe a tension between the model's thresholds for data quality and the data quality that an imperfect, resource-constrained clinic could produce. They argue that this kind of human-centered study should accompany prospective tests of accuracy. Before scaling, pilot in a few real sites and watch the system in use. Measure the workflow (time, rejected inputs, handoffs), not only the model's accuracy on clean validation data.","u":"case-studies.html#thailand-clinics"},{"k":"Section","n":"Case","t":"Case: Succeeds once, fails the next time","x":"Yao et al. introduced τ-bench, which simulates conversations between a user and an agent that has tools and policies to follow, in two domains: retail and airline. They report that even state-of-the-art function-calling agents such as gpt-4o succeed on fewer than 50% of tasks; that figure is an average over the two domains (about 61% in retail and 35% in airline for gpt-4o). They also propose pass^k, the probability that all k attempts at the same task succeed, averaged over tasks, and report that pass^8 is below 25% in the retail domain. Run every test case several times and report the all-runs-pass rate next to the average. A single run per item cannot show whether a success was repeatable.","u":"case-studies.html#tau-bench"},{"k":"Section","n":"Case","t":"Case: A widely used sepsis model, validated locally","x":"Wong et al. (2021) externally validated the original version of the Epic Sepsis Model, a proprietary tool in use at many US hospitals, on 38,455 hospitalizations at Michigan Medicine between December 2018 and October 2019, where sepsis occurred in about 7%. They report a hospitalization-level AUC of 0.63, that the model did not identify about 67% of patients with sepsis, and that, at the alert threshold Michigan Medicine used (a score of 6 or higher), it generated alerts for about 18% of all hospitalized patients. They conclude that it has poor discrimination and calibration. The developer's documentation reported an AUC of 0.76 to 0.83; the authors say the difference is likely due to how they treated the timing of sepsis predictions, and when they also counted scores up to 3 hours after sepsis onset, which the main analysis excluded, the hospitalization-level AUC rose to 0.80. Epic released a revised version of the model in 2022. A 2026 multicenter study of the updated model (Wong et al., JAMA Network Open) reported encounter-level AUROC of 0.82 to 0.92 across four US health systems, with high variability between sites and a high alert burden. Validate on your own site's data, with the metrics and alert threshold fixed in advance, before relying on a vendor's reported performance. Ask how the reported figures handled the timing of predictions and defined sepsis; timing changed the result here. Count the alert burden as well as the hits.","u":"case-studies.html#epic-sepsis"},{"k":"Section","n":"Case","t":"Case: One accuracy number, three commercial classifiers","x":"Buolamwini and Gebru (2018) found that two common face-analysis benchmarks were heavily weighted toward lighter-skinned subjects (79.6% and 86.2%). They built a dataset balanced by gender and skin type and tested three commercial gender classification systems on it in April and May 2017. Error rates were up to 34.7% for darker-skinned women, against a maximum of 0.8% for lighter-skinned men. These figures describe the systems as they were then, not today's versions. Define the groups that matter before looking at the overall score, report results for each, and show the worst one next to the average. Make sure each group has enough examples to carry its own error bars.","u":"case-studies.html#gender-shades"},{"k":"Section","n":"Case","t":"Case: Covid-19 prediction models, reviewed","x":"Wynants et al. (2020) ran a living systematic review of prediction models for diagnosing and predicting the course of covid-19, updated several times. In update 1 (51 studies describing 66 models), all models were rated at high or unclear risk of bias, mostly because of non-representative selection of control patients, exclusion of patients who had not yet had the outcome, a high risk of overfitting, and vague reporting. The authors concluded that the reported performance was probably optimistic and did not recommend any of the models for use in practice. By update 3, the review had screened 37,421 titles and included 169 studies describing 232 models, and the authors report that nearly all were poorly reported and at high risk of bias. Ask whether the data represent the people the model will be used on, whether the model was tested on a cohort it was not built from, and whether the report is complete enough to rerun and audit.","u":"case-studies.html#covid-prediction"},{"k":"Section","n":"Page","t":"Templates for AI evaluation","s":"Vendor questionnaire, eval plan one-pager, and results-reporting template.","u":"templates.html"},{"k":"Section","n":"Template","t":"Template: AI vendor questionnaire","s":"Seventeen questions to send a vendor or internal team before you rely on an AI system, each with what a good answer and a red flag look like. Built from the questions on the pattern pages and the buying-a-model list.","u":"templates.html#vendor-questionnaire"},{"k":"Section","n":"Template","t":"Template: Eval plan one-pager","s":"Fill this in before you run an evaluation: the decision, what is measured, data, locked test set, metrics, baselines, repeats, grader validation, stopping rules, and the owner.","u":"templates.html#eval-plan"},{"k":"Section","n":"Template","t":"Template: Results-reporting template","s":"A layout for reporting results: n, intervals, baselines, breakdowns, cost, and a section on what the evaluation cannot show.","u":"templates.html#results-report"},{"k":"Changelog","n":"19 Oct","t":"Reading aids and a clearer line between the research and this guide’s own framework. Every pattern page now opens with a one-sentence bottom line and its main caveat, which the Share button copies together with the link. A new walkthrough, “A claim, read slowly”, is now chapter 1 of the Guide: it takes three real cases through choosing the claim, testing it and deciding. The About page has a new section on what comes from the research and what is this guide’s own, plus a trust block, and “this guide’s own” markers run through the long-form pages. The Evidence base labels have clearer names. Three patterns have reworded aphorisms, and the rule of thumb for prompt variants, repeated runs and labels is now consistent across pages.","u":"changelog.html"},{"k":"Changelog","n":"19 Oct","t":"Full accuracy re-audit. Every claim on the pattern pages, guide pages, glossary, bibliography, case studies and tools was checked against its primary source. It found five factual errors (a misquoted sentence, a wrong author name, a wrong statistical rule, two experiments reported as one, and a measure described as something it was not) and several dozen statements that were stronger than their sources or missing a caveat. All were corrected, softened or removed, and every changed pattern is now v1.1 with a note in its revision history. The bibliography now counts each work once, the Evidence base and name labels were reviewed (Criteria Drift is now a recent term from one paper, and Functionality Fallacy is a name used here), and the Readiness Review has a scope note on the EU AI Act and NIST references.","u":"changelog.html"},{"k":"Changelog","n":"19 Oct","t":"Review pass. New home page with a Start here block and a clearer Guide hub (the overview essay moved to its own page). Added Sample size and error bars, Real cases, and Templates. Pattern pages now show an Evidence base label and a shorter header on phones. The claim checker has a tested rule set and says plainly when nothing matches. The checklist groups questions by phase and scales to the stakes. Share links now use ?patterns= (old ?laws= links still work). Privacy page now spells out Google Analytics use.","u":"changelog.html"},{"k":"Changelog","n":"7 Oct","t":"The site is now called the AI Evaluation Field Guide. Laws are now called patterns, and pattern pages moved from /laws/ to /patterns/ (old addresses redirect). The old Field Guide page is now the Playbook. The site moved to evalfieldguide.com.","u":"changelog.html"},{"k":"Changelog","n":"7 Oct","t":"Added question lists for buying an AI model or vendor, running a launch review, and building an evaluation, each drawn from the laws.","u":"changelog.html"},{"k":"Changelog","n":"7 Oct","t":"Checked the figures and findings on the law pages against their sources (no discrepancies found) and tested every source link. Added Methodology and Privacy pages, a “Where this doesn’t apply” section on every law, and a “Report it” link for corrections.","u":"changelog.html"},{"k":"Changelog","n":"6 Oct","t":"Added Find your laws: describe your situation with an AI system and get the laws worth checking first, why each applies, and a question to ask.","u":"changelog.html"},{"k":"Changelog","n":"6 Oct","t":"The site now has its own home at lawsofaievaluation.com.","u":"changelog.html"},{"k":"Changelog","n":"5 Oct","t":"Added My laws and the quick brief: collect any laws, then get a short overview built from each law's own text.","u":"changelog.html"},{"k":"Changelog","n":"5 Oct","t":"Launched with all 26 laws, the claim checker, the printable checklist builder, and search.","u":"changelog.html"},{"k":"Changelog","n":"5 Oct","t":"Added the Design Rubric and the Readiness Review as reference pages. Interactive scoring is not built yet.","u":"changelog.html"},{"k":"Page","n":"","t":"Guide to AI evaluation","s":"Where to start and what to read next.","u":"guide.html"},{"k":"Page","n":"","t":"Walkthrough","s":"Three real cases read slowly in three steps: choose the claim, test it against the patterns, decide what you can and cannot conclude.","u":"walkthrough.html"},{"k":"Page","n":"","t":"Overview","s":"What AI evaluation is, why it is hard, and how to use the patterns.","u":"overview.html"},{"k":"Page","n":"","t":"Playbook","s":"Ten questions for reading any AI claim, a sequence for building your own eval, red flags, and an eval card template.","u":"playbook.html"},{"k":"Page","n":"","t":"Being Pragmatic","s":"How to do this inside a real team, with deadlines, a small budget, and a model that changed last Tuesday.","u":"being-pragmatic.html"},{"k":"Page","n":"","t":"Glossary","s":"Definitions of the terms used across the patterns.","u":"glossary.html"},{"k":"Page","n":"","t":"Bibliography","s":"Every source cited on this site, and where each is used.","u":"bibliography.html"},{"k":"Page","n":"","t":"Methodology","s":"How the patterns were chosen, what counts as a source, and what the guide does not claim.","u":"methodology.html"},{"k":"Page","n":"","t":"Questions to ask when buying an AI model or vendor","s":"For choosing or signing off on an AI model, vendor or feature. Questions to put to the people selling it.","u":"questions-to-ask-ai-vendor.html"},{"k":"Page","n":"","t":"Questions for an AI launch review","s":"For the last review before an AI feature ships. Questions about reliability, edge cases and real-world use.","u":"ai-launch-review-questions.html"},{"k":"Page","n":"","t":"Questions to ask when building an AI evaluation","s":"For designing your own evaluation. Questions to check the design before you trust a score.","u":"how-to-build-an-ai-evaluation.html"},{"k":"Page","n":"","t":"Use it now","s":"Questions to ask when someone shares an AI result.","u":"use-it-now.html"},{"k":"Page","n":"","t":"Claim checker","s":"Paste a claim and see which patterns apply.","u":"claim-checker.html"},{"k":"Page","n":"","t":"Find your patterns","s":"Describe your situation and see which patterns to check first.","u":"situation-finder.html"},{"k":"Page","n":"","t":"Quick brief","s":"Pick patterns and get a short overview.","u":"brief.html"},{"k":"Page","n":"","t":"Checklist builder","s":"Build a printable checklist of questions.","u":"checklist.html"}];
